WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:07.430
SCP-173 - It’s one of the most iconic anomalies
ever contained by the SCP Foundation.

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:12.750
This neck-snapping Site-19 celebrity is known
and feared by everyone who works at the Foundation

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:19.550
since being contained in 1993, its nubby,
concrete hands metaphorically, and quite literally,

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.980
stained with the blood of countless people.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:27.480
But this mostly immobile oddity wasn’t always
a resident of Site-19, and the story of how

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.100
exactly it ended up there - and more importantly,
why - is far more ridiculous, insane, and

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.520
downright terrifying than you could ever imagine.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.470
So buckle up, SCP Fans.

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:44.629
This is the dramatic tale of how this homicidal
piece of modern art ended up at the SCP Foundation’s

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:46.219
largest containment site.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:50.410
To give you an idea of just how weird this
origin story is, we’re gonna have to leap

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.410
forward to the year 2045.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:55.809
We know what you’re thinking: Isn’t this
a prequel?

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:58.219
How can it take place in the future?

00:00:58.219 --> 00:01:01.350
Sit tight, folks, it’ll all make sense in
the end.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:07.260
Site-91 is where we lay our scene, under the
watchful eyes of Site Director Dr. James Long,

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.400
a senior researcher with Level 4 Security
Clearance - the second highest at the Foundation.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.810
Dr. Long had given decades of devotion and
his expertise to the Foundation; he was a

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:23.200
proud man, who wore his life of service to
the containment cause as a badge of honor.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.790
After years of dodging death while researching
some of the most dangerous anomalies that

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:31.840
Site-91 had to offer, he had ascended to the
rank of Site Director which represented the

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.950
culmination of his life’s work.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.200
And in his new position of power, he’d only
proved himself further - running a tight ship,

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:42.740
and reducing the number of containment breaches
across the board.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.570
Nobody could say that Dr. Long wasn’t extremely
talented at his job, which is why he was so

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:52.829
confused when he received an email from Alexandra
Nala, an Administrative Assistant working

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:55.310
directly for the O5 Council.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:02:00.370
It was the type of email that no Site Director
ever wanted to receive: Dr. Long was being

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:05.540
told that, with no input or oversight from
him, the majority of Site-91’s anomalies

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.009
were going to be taken to an undisclosed location
for “Safer Containment.”

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.370
The list contained nearly every meaningful
detainee contained on the site, including

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.730
of course, SCP-173.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:22.340
This particular anomaly had been contained
at the site for 37 years after being delivered

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.040
there in 2008.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.150
Dr. Long had devised a perfect method to minimize
containment breaches in that time - creating

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.530
a sprinkler system within the chamber that
washes away the waste products produced by

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.550
173, reducing the need for risky human contact.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.290
But that didn’t matter.

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:44.349
Despite Dr. Long’s loyalty and efficacy
in serving the SCP Foundation, the decision

00:02:44.349 --> 00:02:48.480
had already been made, with no chance of reversing
it.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.040
According to Nala, the motion to remove most
of Site-91’s anomalies had received unanimous

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.819
approval from the O5 Council, and the operations
for the transfer would be commandeered by

00:02:57.819 --> 00:03:02.599
the Department of Extra-Universal Affairs,
headed by Senior Agent Sven Kisch.

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.989
Dr. Long was both upset and confused.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:10.530
He was producing some of the best results
across the entire Foundation, so why were

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.010
they taking away all of his anomalies?

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.860
Did someone on the O5 Council have it out
for him?

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:21.060
Dr. Long replied to Nala, asking if - considering
how severe the relocation operation was going

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:25.810
to be - whether he could get more information
on where the anomalies were being moved to,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.810
and why.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.379
He felt that, at the very least, he was owed
some answers on this.

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:34.090
Nala and her superiors didn’t feel quite
the same way.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.730
She replied that Dr. Long sadly didn’t have
the clearance to know any of the operation’s

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.540
particulars - this information was reserved
for those with Level 5 Clearance, and members

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:45.879
of the Multi-Universal Department.

00:03:45.879 --> 00:03:49.930
All that Dr. Long needed to do was stay out
of the way, and everything would be taken

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.370
care of for him.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.129
But that wasn’t enough for Dr. Long.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:55.510
He needed to know more.

00:03:55.510 --> 00:04:00.330
He needed to understand why this seemingly
random decision had been made.

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.769
Realizing that Nala and the O5 Council were
a dead end as far as information was concerned,

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:10.730
he instead reached out to the next best thing:
Senior Agent Sven Kisch, the member of the

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:15.409
Department of Extra-Universal Affairs who
was spearheading the relocation mission.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:20.250
Dr. Long implored the Agent to give him extra
information on where all the Site-91 anomalies

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:24.350
were being moved, and why they were being
moved in the first place.

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:28.490
Predictably, he was once again stonewalled,
being told that any information regarding

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.789
the project was above his level of clearance.

00:04:30.789 --> 00:04:35.600
In a move that seemed decidedly spiteful,
Agent Kisch signed off by saying that he looked

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.949
forward to seeing Dr. Long at Site-91.

00:04:38.949 --> 00:04:41.600
And not long after that, he did.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:46.000
Agent Kisch along with a huge number of agents
and task force members from the Multi-U Department

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:49.600
arrived on site, bearing state of the art
containment equipment.

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.699
An exasperated Dr. Long wasn’t able to glean
any more information from this experience

00:04:54.699 --> 00:04:59.580
- the Multi-U agents were ruthlessly efficient,
transferring the lion’s share of the anomalies

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.930
into temporary containment and transporting
them off-site within a few hours.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:09.060
By the time they were done, Dr. Long and his
staff were left with one of the most desolate

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:12.800
and empty containment sites on the Foundation’s
books.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:18.180
Here was Dr. Long, with decades of service,
and climbing the organizational ladder, only

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:23.430
to be left as a glorified babysitter for a
handful of low-priority anomalous items.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:25.810
It all felt like a cruel joke.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:31.060
After several days of nothing happening in
the now-incredibly-uneventful Site-91, a bored

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:38.120
Dr. Long reached out to a friend of his - Researcher
Nurul Shafiqah binte Ahmad Ibrahim, whom he

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:41.539
called by the nickname Shaq - to vent his
frustrations.

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:46.949
She commiserated, sharing her sympathy for
his suddenly much less exciting working conditions,

00:05:46.949 --> 00:05:50.120
but saying that at least the job should be
a little less lethal now.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:55.550
Shaq didn’t know it yet, but on this particular
point, she was terribly wrong.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:00.360
Dr. Long shared his theory that perhaps some
sites were being consolidated so the Foundation

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:03.650
could save money, and that this would lead
to layoffs.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.620
Shaq essentially told him that he was worrying
too much.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:11.490
The reality was that Dr. Long had some very
good reasons to be worried, his problem was

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.730
that he hadn’t been worrying about the right
things.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:19.580
In Foundation Containment Area-179, something
awful was brewing.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:26.060
As usual, guards posted around SCP-2317 - an
old door containing a portal to another world

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:31.030
- waited while Foundation staff and D-Classes
performed the standard Procedure 220-Calabasas

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:32.740
ritual within.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:37.180
They were dealing with one of the most dangerous
anomalies in the entire universe, but even

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:41.800
then, after decades of service, one can become
numb to such things.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:46.310
Suddenly, without warning, the door opened
and a terrified Foundation researcher tumbled

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:49.979
out, panting heavily, eyes bulging in existential
horror.

00:06:49.979 --> 00:06:54.919
He screamed the words that he had long hoped
would never be spoken in his or even his children’s

00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:55.940
lifetime, “It happened!

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.110
The chains have broken!

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.810
The Devourer is free!”

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:05.770
The siren sounded, and an alert spread out
across the entire Foundation database: SCP-2317

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:12.410
is compromised, SCP-2317-K - the Devourer
of Worlds - is free.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:18.520
If the creature escapes Area-179, an XK-Class
End of the World Scenario is basically assured,

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:23.330
so all efforts will be taken to make sure
that the creature does not escape at any cost.

00:07:23.330 --> 00:07:26.460
And they really did mean any cost.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:33.229
The old wooden door of SCP-2317 splintered,
as an immense, scaly hand sprang forth, and

00:07:33.229 --> 00:07:36.770
clawed a wave of devastation through the containment
chamber.

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:41.199
Guards were dispatched in the hundreds with
heavy weaponry to buy some time, firing as

00:07:41.199 --> 00:07:45.900
the Devourer, a beast the size of a mountain
rose from the ground, staring at the terrified

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.190
humans below with its one gigantic red eye.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:54.889
It couldn’t even feel the bullets, explosives,
or incendiary and laser weaponry being used

00:07:54.889 --> 00:07:55.889
on it.

00:07:55.889 --> 00:08:00.740
The monster just shrugged it off, and murdered
nearly all its attackers with one swipe of

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.759
its claw.

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:07.479
In a final desperate act, hoping to kill or
at least slow down the beast, staff at Area-179

00:08:07.479 --> 00:08:12.710
made the ultimate sacrifice, and detonated
the entire on-site nuclear arsenal.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:17.509
The explosion disintegrated the entire facility
in an instant, and unleashed a radioactive

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:20.690
shockwave that carried destruction for miles.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:23.449
But it wasn’t enough.

00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:28.350
SCP-2317-K walked out of the mushroom cloud
without a scratch on it, ready to show the

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.650
human world what real destruction meant.

00:08:31.650 --> 00:08:36.620
At this point, the battle was already effectively
lost, but the Foundation refused to go down

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:37.830
without a fight.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:42.830
They dispatched all available mobile task
forces - including Mobile Task Force Nu-7,

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:47.730
Hammer Down, a heavily-armed, battalion-strength
task force created to combat the biggest and

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.560
most extensive threats imaginable.

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:54.180
And it wasn’t even just the Foundation - true
existential threats have a way of uniting

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:59.800
people, as the GOC and even the Chaos Insurgency
temporarily put aside their differences to

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.700
take the fight to the Devourer.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:03.820
But would any of it be enough?

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:08.320
Barrages of cruise missiles and ICBMs did
nothing to halt the Devourer’s assault,

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:14.570
as it rampaged, destroying towns and cities
at first, then regions, then whole nations.

00:09:14.570 --> 00:09:19.200
Swarms of scrambled jets and helicopters were
knocked out of the sky with casual swipes.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.700
The creature bulldozed over landscapes teeming
with tanks and heavy artillery vehicles.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.579
It snapped aircraft carriers in half as it
waded from ocean to ocean, systematically

00:09:28.579 --> 00:09:31.399
destroying everything in its path.

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:36.360
Even satellite-mounted orbital cannons and
the heavy energy weapons of MTF Tau-5, also

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.240
known as "Samsara", did nothing to faze the
Devourer.

00:09:40.240 --> 00:09:46.380
It was finally coming to fruition: The dreaded
XK Class End of the World Scenario, and none

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:51.390
of the Foundation’s expertise, advanced
technology, or limitless resources could do

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.019
anything to stop it.

00:09:53.019 --> 00:09:57.600
They aided in evacuation efforts, getting
as many people as they could out of harm’s

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.110
way as the Devourer rampaged and destroyed,
but even that was doing little more than borrowing

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:04.110
time.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.550
Eventually, the Devourer would destroy all
of them.

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:08.720
There would be nothing left.

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:14.610
And all Dr. James Long could do was watch
helplessly from an almost empty Site-91, waiting

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:16.721
for the tide of destruction to reach him.

00:10:16.721 --> 00:10:21.930
Of course, other Foundation employees - most
prominently, Senior Agent Sven Kisch and the

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.360
Multi-U Department - had other concerns.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:27.700
All they needed to do was archive data for
extraction.

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:31.310
Extraction to where, you ask, seeing as the
world seems to be at its end here?

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:38.630
Well, Universe 7392-Epsilon-Blue-Lima, otherwise
known as our universe, circa 1993.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:42.899
The Foundation works across the multiverse,
and an XK-Class End of the World Scenario

00:10:42.899 --> 00:10:46.870
in one universe really isn’t necessarily
a problem in others.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:51.089
On the orders of the O5 Council, Agent Kisch
and his men are charged with extracting data

00:10:51.089 --> 00:10:55.310
and valuable anomalies like SCP-173 from doomed
universes.

00:10:55.310 --> 00:11:02.190
O5 knew that Dr. Long’s universe - Universe
5643-Gamma-Orange-Delta - was due for an apocalyptic

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.800
visit from the Devourer of Worlds any day
now.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.019
That’s why the Multi-U team went in to extract
everything worth saving.

00:11:09.019 --> 00:11:13.880
Dr. Long and the millions of other Level-4
and below employees of that universe’s Foundation

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:16.899
were never told of their impending doom.

00:11:16.899 --> 00:11:20.790
After all, that sort of thing could probably
lead to unnecessary stress.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:25.630
And what they don’t know won’t hurt them
- until, of course, it does.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:31.750
In 1993, these anomalous, doomed universe
SCPs were divided among our universe’s various

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.130
applicable containment sites.

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:38.420
SCP-173, as you already know, ended up in
Site-19.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:42.899
But sadly for the people working there, all
the actual data on this entity’s file was

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:48.980
lost in translation, so as far as they knew,
173 was little more than a big, weird statue

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:51.750
with apparently dangerous properties.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:57.010
Upon first placing it in its containment chamber
in the Site-19 Euclid Wing, Foundation legend

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:02.440
Dr. Bright, unaware of just what he had here,
assigned Dr. William Motto to begin research

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:03.570
on the entity.

00:12:03.570 --> 00:12:07.060
He recommended that the Researcher bring two
D-Classes for testing.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:11.610
When Dr. Motto was faced with the anomalous
entity’s blank file, he gave a sigh, and

00:12:11.610 --> 00:12:17.570
began to write something in its description
box: “Moved to Site-19 1993.

00:12:17.570 --> 00:12:19.170
Origin is as of yet unknown…”

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:21.990
Wasn’t that a delightful little tale?

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:28.070
Now we know how SCP-173 made its way into
Site-19, and what our own universe may have

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:31.019
to look forward to in 2045.

00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:36.120
So remember, if you ever see Agent Sven Kisch
planning a transfer of anomalies out of your

00:12:36.120 --> 00:12:41.570
site, maybe consider using some of that PTO,
and maybe consider putting a downpayment on

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.589
a nice, cozy apocalypse bunker over in Iceland…

00:12:44.589 --> 00:12:47.060
Know of other tales you’d like to see us
cover?

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:49.040
Let us know down in the comments below!

00:12:49.040 --> 00:12:54.540
The SCP Foundation specializes in tracking
down and containing anomalies that are difficult

00:12:54.540 --> 00:13:00.260
to find, and that often will do anything they
can to avoid being found.

00:13:00.260 --> 00:13:05.940
One anomaly in particular hates being seen
more than anything, and will punish anyone

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:09.589
who looks at its face with a horrific fate.

00:13:09.589 --> 00:13:15.790
SCP-096 is a mysterious creature, not only
because it destroys anyone who sees what it

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:16.830
truly looks like.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:23.800
No one is entirely certain where it came from,
what exactly it is, or why it reacts so violently

00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:24.990
to being perceived.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:31.040
There are, however, several theories as to
the Shy Guy's origin, and none of them are

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.380
particularly cheerful.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:37.829
Some suggest an experiment gone wrong, others
believe it to be the last remaining member

00:13:37.829 --> 00:13:41.050
of an unknown species of mountain-dwelling
humanoids.

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:48.040
But one theory about SCP-096's origin stands
out as particularly tragic in nature.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:54.480
In this story, SCP-096 was not always a long-limbed,
pale beast cowering in the corner and hiding

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.250
its face from the world.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:14:01.240
It was once an ordinary man, like anyone you
might pass on the street.

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:07.040
He was someone's son, someone's friend, a
person with hopes, dreams, and passions.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:14.389
A human being who, through anomalous circumstances
beyond his control, was made into a monster.

00:14:14.389 --> 00:14:17.190
Many years ago, there was a mountaineer named
Jack.

00:14:17.190 --> 00:14:22.160
All his adult life, he had been obsessed with
reaching higher and higher peaks, climbing

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:27.759
every mountain he could in order to test his
limits, pit himself against the power of nature,

00:14:27.759 --> 00:14:31.579
and bask in the sweet reward that was the
view from the top.

00:14:31.579 --> 00:14:37.550
It began as a sport, a way to build up strength
while taking in incredible natural beauty,

00:14:37.550 --> 00:14:43.440
but over time as he conquered increasingly
difficult challenges, it became an obsession.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:48.230
Like every obsession eventually does, climbing
mountains began to take over his life, driving

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:49.899
everything else away.

00:14:49.899 --> 00:14:55.070
He struggled to hold down a job, his marriage
fell apart, and his relationships to his friends

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:58.880
and family became increasingly distant and
strained.

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.810
Still, he couldn't bring himself to care much.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:06.110
He spent his time alone in the wilderness,
or every so often embarking on trips with

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:07.710
other mountaineers.

00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:13.170
Whether he was going at it solo or working
with others, he was living for the climb.

00:15:13.170 --> 00:15:19.060
Over the years, he took on Cerro Torre in
Argentina, The Eiger in Switzerland, and Denali

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:20.080
in Alaska.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:25.200
He especially enjoyed climbing in extreme
cold, braving the ice and snow as he embarked

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:26.640
on his climbs.

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:31.290
The few fellow mountaineers he kept up with
gave him the nickname "Jack Frost."

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:35.770
After years of refining his craft, building
his strength, he set his sights on the highest

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:39.910
mountain in the world, his personal white
whale: Mt.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:41.579
Everest itself.

00:15:41.579 --> 00:15:46.130
It was one of the deadliest climbs in the
world, with its staggering height, storms,

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:50.060
and frozen graveyard of fallen climbers dotting
the snowy surface.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:52.089
But he was determined.

00:15:52.089 --> 00:15:56.759
He would not join the ranks of the fallen,
but instead, be one of the victorious that

00:15:56.759 --> 00:16:00.759
reached the top of Everest and came back down
in one piece.

00:16:00.759 --> 00:16:03.170
He could feel it in his bones.

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:08.010
Jack arrived in Nepal with a pack full of
climbing equipment, the best boots money could

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:13.430
buy, and layers upon layers of clothing to
keep himself warm on the journey to the summit.

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:18.949
He lowered his goggles over his eyes, summoned
his courage, and prepared for the incredibly

00:16:18.949 --> 00:16:20.490
difficult climb.

00:16:20.490 --> 00:16:26.010
The cold seeped into his core, the winds nipped
at his face, numbing his nose and leeching

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:30.290
through his gloves and boots as he went, but
Jack pressed on.

00:16:30.290 --> 00:16:33.069
He welcomed the sensation, the bite of it.

00:16:33.069 --> 00:16:38.700
It was a reminder that he was still here,
that he was still alive.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:42.370
As long as he could feel the pain, he knew
he was alright.

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:47.779
He knew that it was when the cold faded away,
turned to paradoxical feelings of warmth and

00:16:47.779 --> 00:16:51.009
sleepiness, that a mountaineer was truly in
trouble.

00:16:51.009 --> 00:16:56.220
As he navigated the harsh mountain landscape,
Jack occasionally let his eyes drift off course,

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:58.110
taking in the sights around him.

00:16:58.110 --> 00:17:03.470
He could see shocks of blue, green, and red,
the distinct bright colors of boots, jackets,

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:07.520
and hats belonging to the fallen climbers
that came before him.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:12.319
Those who collapsed from exhaustion, who gave
in to altitude sickness or succumbed to the

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:13.319
cold.

00:17:13.319 --> 00:17:14.319
"Memento Mori."

00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:16.579
He said softly to himself.

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:22.240
These frozen bodies represented that ever-present
reminder of mortality, of death lurking just

00:17:22.240 --> 00:17:23.350
out of sight.

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:25.720
It would not come for him though.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:27.939
Not today, not on this journey.

00:17:27.939 --> 00:17:29.309
He wouldn't let it.

00:17:29.309 --> 00:17:33.860
The hours blurred together within the crunch
of the ice beneath his boots, the dig of climbing

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:38.840
hooks into the earth, the tug of his trusty
ropes as he ascended ever higher.

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.250
Jack came to a stop.

00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:44.440
There was no next step to take, nowhere higher
that he could go.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.030
He had reached the summit.

00:17:46.030 --> 00:17:50.000
The white whale was captured, and Everest
was his.

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:54.299
For the very first time, he was truly on top
of the world.

00:17:54.299 --> 00:17:59.730
Now that he had made it to the highest point,
there was the all-important task of climbing

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:00.940
back down.

00:18:00.940 --> 00:18:05.490
At this altitude, he was still in the "death
zone," the place where human acclimatization

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:07.520
was impossible.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:11.770
This was the tricky part, the point at which
even the most experienced of climbers might

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:16.770
get lost in the euphoria of the achievement
might let their attention slip.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:23.010
Any mistake during his descent, any misstep,
could be the thing that cost him his life.

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:26.549
There was no room for error and no time to
waste.

00:18:26.549 --> 00:18:32.000
Just as Jack prepared to make his descent,
he saw a figure pulling itself over the ridge.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.549
From a distance, it looked like a fellow climber.

00:18:34.549 --> 00:18:39.020
He noted the curious lack of equipment, and
the way the person seemed to be making their

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:43.980
way up the mountain with nothing but their
hands, but perhaps they had lost their pack

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:46.150
or left it behind for some reason.

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:50.890
The figure was dressed in all white from their
hood to their boots, with a large pair of

00:18:50.890 --> 00:18:55.910
black mountain goggles covering what little
of their face wasn't concealed by the hood.

00:18:55.910 --> 00:19:00.659
Uncertain if the figure was in need of assistance,
and surprised to see another soul on the mountain

00:19:00.659 --> 00:19:03.260
with him, Jack waved at the figure.

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:04.419
"Everything okay?"

00:19:04.419 --> 00:19:05.590
He called.

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:08.930
There was no response, but the figure turned
to look at him.

00:19:08.930 --> 00:19:11.460
At least, he thought it was looking at him.

00:19:11.460 --> 00:19:16.440
He couldn't see its eyes through the goggles,
but he felt the distinct sensation of it locking

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:19.240
its gaze with his, just the same.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:23.890
As soon as the figure turned to look at him,
Jack began to feel strange.

00:19:23.890 --> 00:19:28.929
The pain, the sharp sensation of the cold
that he relied on to ground himself and remember

00:19:28.929 --> 00:19:32.960
he was really here, began to blur around the
edges.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:38.159
The feeling softened, melting away along with
the ache in his muscles, the bleariness in

00:19:38.159 --> 00:19:42.290
his eyes, the dry mouth that had nagged at
him for hours.

00:19:42.290 --> 00:19:47.320
He felt unbalanced, unmoored, a ship with
no anchor.

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:51.820
He couldn't explain why, but the sudden relief
from the cold made his chest tighten with

00:19:51.820 --> 00:19:52.880
primal terror.

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:57.120
He did not feel like one man encountering
another on the mountain.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:02.580
He felt the way he had years ago when he came
face to face with a mountain lion, staring

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:07.690
down a predator and remembering all of a sudden
that humans do not naturally sit at the top

00:20:07.690 --> 00:20:09.330
of the food chain.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:13.409
This figure, this thing...it was no man.

00:20:13.409 --> 00:20:16.309
It was no person.

00:20:16.309 --> 00:20:20.700
Jack prepared to continue his descent, to
get away from the strange intruder as quickly

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:24.169
as possible, but his feet would not obey him.

00:20:24.169 --> 00:20:28.059
His eyes could not break contact with the
figure in white that was slowly beginning

00:20:28.059 --> 00:20:32.110
to advance toward him, one slow step at a
time.

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:33.350
It was in no hurry.

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:36.970
It knew he was not going anywhere any time
soon.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:42.070
He trembled as the figure drew closer, its
inscrutable face hidden beneath its hood and

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:43.350
behind its goggles.

00:20:43.350 --> 00:20:48.430
He wished he could see its face, but dreaded
learning what secrets it was hiding.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:53.250
If only he could just unstick his feet from
the earth, break free of this spell that had

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.730
fallen over him, he could escape.

00:20:55.730 --> 00:21:01.440
But no matter how his muscles strained, how
he gritted his teeth with effort, he couldn't

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:02.480
get away.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:08.170
As the entity came closer, the cold snapped
back into his body- but something was different.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:13.940
It was heightened, like every icy breeze and
freezing wind layered on top of itself, hardening

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:18.760
his muscles to rock, crystallizing the very
blood in his veins.

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:24.000
He felt as if his fingers, if he tried to
wriggle them, would snap off and fall to the

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.000
ground.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:29.710
His eyes thrummed in their sockets, his lungs
struggling to hold onto each breath as the

00:21:29.710 --> 00:21:32.750
cold constricted its fist around his chest.

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:38.720
He opened his mouth to speak but his voice
died in his throat, coming out in a weak whisper

00:21:38.720 --> 00:21:39.720
of air.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:44.600
Now, the thing was close enough to touch him,
and whatever force had been holding him still

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.660
and upright released him.

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:51.600
It was as if he were a marionette suspended
in the air, and the puppeteer released his

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:53.420
strings all at once.

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:58.190
He crumpled to the snow-covered ground in
a heap, his cheek pressed into the earth,

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:02.120
his eyes trained helplessly on the still-advancing
figure.

00:22:02.120 --> 00:22:07.700
His lips formed the word "please..." though
he could not tell exactly what he was begging

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:08.700
for.

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:09.700
His life?

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:10.700
His freedom?

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:12.400
A painless death?

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:17.220
He struggled to push himself to his feet,
but all the strength had gone out of his limbs.

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:21.240
He could barely lift his arm, let alone handle
the weight of his whole body.

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:26.750
The thing shaped like a man was right above
him, glowering down through those opaque goggles.

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:32.890
He still could not see its eyes, but somehow
he could feel the way it was looking at him.

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:38.690
Hatred was radiating off of it, disdain and
pure, unadulterated malice.

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:45.380
Then, it tilted its head to the side thoughtfully,
and Jack's mind was overwhelmed with images.

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:50.590
He felt the phantom sensation of sunlight
on his skin, and springy green grass beneath

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:51.590
his bare feet.

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:57.320
He could see, clear as day in his mind's eye,
a forest clearing in the middle of summer,

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:02.510
dewdrops sparkling on the petals of wildflowers
blowing in a gentle, sweet breeze.

00:23:02.510 --> 00:23:08.760
Then, he felt sand between his toes, smelled
the sea breeze, felt the comfort and contentment

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:09.960
of a nap on the beach.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:16.770
It was not offering these things to him, but
something like them...something like peace.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.429
It was offering him...a gift.

00:23:19.429 --> 00:23:20.730
But he didn't want it.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:21.730
He couldn't take it.

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:26.860
He was Jack Frost, dammit, he thrived in the
cold, the hardship.

00:23:26.860 --> 00:23:30.260
He didn't want some warm, sleepy fade out
of existence.

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:35.300
If he went out, it would be on his terms,
doing the thing he had dedicated his life

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:36.300
to.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:40.240
If he were going to lose his life, he wouldn't
roll over and just give it up.

00:23:40.240 --> 00:23:43.770
It would need to be pried from his frozen
hands.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:48.799
With every last bit of strength, he twisted
his head away from the entity's gaze, covering

00:23:48.799 --> 00:23:49.929
his face with his hands.

00:23:49.929 --> 00:23:54.990
It grabbed at him, shaking his shoulders,
trying to force him to uncover his face and

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.460
meet its gaze once more, but he didn't budge.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:23:59.460
He wouldn't.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:00.460
He couldn't.

00:24:00.460 --> 00:24:05.710
Then, he felt the energy shift as it realized
he had refused the gift, that he would not

00:24:05.710 --> 00:24:11.130
welcome the paradoxical feelings of heat and
security and slip away as it was asking him

00:24:11.130 --> 00:24:12.130
to.

00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:14.020
But it was not a victory.

00:24:14.020 --> 00:24:25.480
He swore he could hear a voice in his mind
say, "Very well...you wish to be part of the

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:26.480
mountain?

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:27.480
I will make it so."

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:30.669
The world went white as Jack's eyes rolled
back in his head, his spine curving back until

00:24:30.669 --> 00:24:31.669
it snapped.

00:24:31.669 --> 00:24:37.200
His jaw cracked and distended, his arms and
legs stretched out long and twisted, his body

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:42.809
contorted at unnatural angles as the entity
began to rearrange his very being.

00:24:42.809 --> 00:24:47.710
As the agony ripped through him, snapping
his bones and tearing his muscles, Jack kept

00:24:47.710 --> 00:24:49.920
his face cradled in his hands.

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:55.350
He remained in that position even as he cried
his voice hoarse, as the pure fear and pain

00:24:55.350 --> 00:25:02.049
overwhelmed his senses, until he forgot his
own name, forget he was ever a man at all.

00:25:02.049 --> 00:25:07.280
When Jack was no more, when something else
sat there in his place, pale and shivering,

00:25:07.280 --> 00:25:13.190
body wracked with sobs, the King of the Mountain
turned and left him there, alone with his

00:25:13.190 --> 00:25:14.190
misery.

00:25:14.190 --> 00:25:16.510
Still, he would not uncover his face.

00:25:16.510 --> 00:25:21.190
He would not let himself be vulnerable, would
not look up.

00:25:21.190 --> 00:25:25.130
Days later, a group of mountaineers reached
the summit of Everest and spotted a hairless

00:25:25.130 --> 00:25:27.770
figure crouched in the snow, weeping softly.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:29.419
"Are you alright?"

00:25:29.419 --> 00:25:30.460
One of them asked.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:34.240
The figure lifted its head slowly, and locked
eyes with the strangers.

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:41.590
Its eyes widened in horror, its mouth stretched
impossibly wide in a wordless scream of rage

00:25:41.590 --> 00:25:48.029
and hurt, and, with nothing human left in
its mind or heart, SCP-096 took its very first

00:25:48.029 --> 00:25:49.750
victim.

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:54.159
Robed figures with hidden faces stand over
seven young women, tied up.

00:25:54.159 --> 00:25:58.010
The robed figures chant phrases both arcane
and profane.

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:03.260
The leader among these dark cultists clears
his wicked throat and says, “Be not afraid,

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:04.260
girls.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:07.320
You are here for something wonderful.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:11.970
To beget seven perfect children for the Scarlet
King…”

00:26:11.970 --> 00:26:16.330
How did we get here, and more importantly,
what happened afterward?

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:21.650
In the near-constant darkness that follows
the grim but necessary work of the SCP Foundation,

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:26.309
there is one bright light that can always
be counted on to keep hope alive, to bring

00:26:26.309 --> 00:26:32.610
a little sunshine into everyone's days: SCP-999,
the Tickle Monster.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:37.360
The sweet, simple creature does nothing but
provide an endless source of joy, hugging,

00:26:37.360 --> 00:26:40.500
playing, and delighting in loving everything
around it.

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:44.740
But what if I told you that this slimy little
friend has hidden depths?

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:49.750
A tragic history that seems impossible to
reconcile with the happy, playful being we

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:51.270
all know and love?

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:55.820
How could 999, the happiest SCP ever, have
a sad backstory?

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:59.990
Well, it's one of the best-kept secrets at
the Foundation.

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:04.309
Before we get to that, let's talk about a
Foundation researcher and her brand new job

00:27:04.309 --> 00:27:10.110
- a new job that would make her privy to some
highly classified Foundation secrets.

00:27:10.110 --> 00:27:14.820
Dr. Izzy Collingwood was delighted to wake
up to a call informing her that she had been

00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:19.480
appointed to one of the most coveted positions
at the SCP Foundation.

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:23.559
After applying, crossing her fingers, and
hoping for the best, she had been promoted

00:27:23.559 --> 00:27:27.250
to Head Researcher for SCP-999.

00:27:27.250 --> 00:27:32.000
What could be better than attending to the
kindest, gentlest, cutest anomaly in Foundation

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:33.010
custody?

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:36.750
Gone were the days of coming into work and
wondering if she'd leave in one piece.

00:27:36.750 --> 00:27:42.049
No more cleaning up toxic spills, fighting
to contain killer creatures, or fetching cups

00:27:42.049 --> 00:27:46.649
of coffee for old doctors that wouldn't give
her so much as a "thank you."

00:27:46.649 --> 00:27:48.620
Years of hard work had paid off.

00:27:48.620 --> 00:27:52.549
Now, she could rest on her laurels and do
work that she truly enjoyed.

00:27:52.549 --> 00:27:57.860
There was just the matter of some simple paperwork,
and she would be ready to begin this extremely

00:27:57.860 --> 00:28:01.279
welcome new chapter of her professional life.

00:28:01.279 --> 00:28:05.450
When she walked into her office to get started
on the paperwork, something unusual caught

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.450
her eye.

00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:11.559
There was a small stack of files on her desk,
with a sticky note attached to the front.

00:28:11.559 --> 00:28:17.020
There were just two words written on the note,
to indicate what might await her inside the

00:28:17.020 --> 00:28:18.020
folders: "The Truth."

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:19.080
She scoffed at first.

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.130
"Very funny, guys!"

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:22.370
She called out the door.

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:27.120
But no one responded, no one came forward
to take credit for the elaborate joke.

00:28:27.120 --> 00:28:31.580
Maybe they were playing the long game, or
maybe... she couldn't be sure.

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:34.150
She sat down, flipping the first folder open.

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:38.110
It was untitled, unlabeled, just plain text
on a page.

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:43.380
She almost closed it after a brief scan, but
the words "The Scarlet King" jumped out at

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:44.380
her.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:48.350
She had worked at the Foundation long enough
to hear that name more than a few times, often

00:28:48.350 --> 00:28:54.580
spoken in hushed tones, as if merely speaking
his name would bring about some kind of disaster.

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:59.510
As she dug deeper into the file, she realized
just what she was looking at: the proposed

00:28:59.510 --> 00:29:02.720
origins of the Scarlet King himself.

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:08.809
It was a folkloric explanation, one her scientific
mind struggled to wrap around, but she had

00:29:08.809 --> 00:29:14.320
seen so many strange things over the years,
things that defied all rational explanation.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:17.880
Perhaps there was some truth to be found in
the myths.

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:22.730
The Scarlet King had, according to the file,
once been an elder god born from the earliest

00:29:22.730 --> 00:29:26.080
days of existence, in the shadows of the Tree
of Knowledge.

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:31.870
He had devoured his brothers and sisters,
growing stronger with each one he consumed.

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:37.549
As his strength increased, so too did his
overwhelming pain and hate, his deep desire

00:29:37.549 --> 00:29:41.559
to destroy the Tree of Knowledge and all living
creatures.

00:29:41.559 --> 00:29:46.320
He declared himself the king of darkness,
and, through a series of horrific events,

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:51.919
took seven unwilling brides and bound them
to him with magical seals.

00:29:51.919 --> 00:29:57.429
Each bride bore supernatural children, powerful,
terrible, and monstrous.

00:29:57.429 --> 00:30:01.230
The first bride, A'tivik, had only a few children.

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:06.110
Those few were skilled strategists, exceptionally
wise in the ways of war.

00:30:06.110 --> 00:30:10.899
The second bride was A'ghor, overcome with
constant despair and desolation.

00:30:10.899 --> 00:30:16.530
Her children conquered with massive, unthinking
armies, never sated and never quelled.

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:21.320
The third bride, A'distat, hated her sisters
and all she surveyed.

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:25.179
The fourth bride, A'zieb, took the shape of
a great beast.

00:30:25.179 --> 00:30:30.650
Her children followed in her stead, and were
invulnerable, their thick hides impenetrable

00:30:30.650 --> 00:30:33.269
by physical or magical means.

00:30:33.269 --> 00:30:38.250
The fifth bride was A'nuht, blessed with a
strong mind and cursed with a fragile body.

00:30:38.250 --> 00:30:42.510
Her children were gifted in the ways of magic,
and incredibly destructive.

00:30:42.510 --> 00:30:45.769
The sixth bride, A'tellif, would not speak.

00:30:45.769 --> 00:30:50.970
She kept to herself, and, like her, her children
moved in silence, disguising themselves as

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:52.340
whatever they wished.

00:30:52.340 --> 00:30:55.010
The seventh bride, however, was different.

00:30:55.010 --> 00:31:00.140
A'habbat was her name, the smallest of the
seven, with an unbroken spirit.

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:05.190
She birthed mighty heroes that she trained
in secret, in hopes that one day they would

00:31:05.190 --> 00:31:09.940
overcome her family's darkness, and topple
the King from his throne.

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:14.690
All the while, the Scarlet King wages his
unholy war against all things, waiting for

00:31:14.690 --> 00:31:19.450
the day he can arrive in our realm and unleash
absolute destruction.

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:24.620
Dr. Collingwood shuddered as she finished
reading, an undeniable chill passing over

00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:25.620
her.

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:28.970
This definitely wasn't some kind of prank
or hazing ritual.

00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:30.960
This was something dark.

00:31:30.960 --> 00:31:31.970
Something real.

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:35.830
She held secrets in her hands, and she couldn't
stop reading them now.

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:37.710
She opened the next folder.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:42.820
It was a description of a cult, monitored
and raided by the SCP Foundation, called "the

00:31:42.820 --> 00:31:47.179
Children of the Scarlet King," also known
as Group MENDES.

00:31:47.179 --> 00:31:51.399
During a sixteen-month investigation of the
anomalous religious group, the Foundation's

00:31:51.399 --> 00:31:56.039
undercover operatives were able to ascertain
a great deal of pertinent information on the

00:31:56.039 --> 00:31:57.039
group.

00:31:57.039 --> 00:32:00.000
What they found was troubling, and of great
concern.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:06.159
The group had ties to several wealthy benefactors,
as well as links to extremist paranormal groups.

00:32:06.159 --> 00:32:10.990
Their set of beliefs was relatively simple
and disturbing: they worshipped the Scarlet

00:32:10.990 --> 00:32:16.409
King, and were devoted to supporting him in
his battle to destroy the Earth and all life

00:32:16.409 --> 00:32:17.620
as we know it.

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:22.520
The faithful who followed him in this task
would be rewarded handsomely with positions

00:32:22.520 --> 00:32:25.049
of power in the new, hellish world.

00:32:25.049 --> 00:32:30.289
An initial estimate put the cult's membership
at approximately eight thousand members, a

00:32:30.289 --> 00:32:33.809
quarter of whom belonged to the group's primary
branch in Alabama.

00:32:33.809 --> 00:32:38.799
The reporting on the cult was detailed, far
too dense, and heavily researched to be fake.

00:32:38.799 --> 00:32:43.429
Again, she had the distinct feeling that someone
was trying to tip her off about something

00:32:43.429 --> 00:32:46.690
the Foundation hadn't wanted to be widespread
knowledge.

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:49.760
With shaky hands, she picked up the final
file.

00:32:49.760 --> 00:32:51.080
What awaited her inside?

00:32:51.080 --> 00:32:53.990
There was only one way to find out.

00:32:53.990 --> 00:32:56.730
She flipped it open, and began to read.

00:32:56.730 --> 00:33:02.880
It was a file on an entity known as SCP-231,
specifically SCP-231-7.

00:33:02.880 --> 00:33:08.740
An addendum was attached to the beginning
of the file, reading: "All personnel assigned

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:15.669
to SCP-231-7 must rotate out for one month
of psychological counseling after two months

00:33:15.669 --> 00:33:16.669
on-site.

00:33:16.669 --> 00:33:21.340
SCP-231-7 is to be kept at an undisclosed
location.

00:33:21.340 --> 00:33:26.710
All personnel assigned to SCP-231 will be
transported there blindfolded from Site-19

00:33:26.710 --> 00:33:31.730
by a route including no fewer than seven different
forms of transportation, including but not

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:38.159
limited to aircraft, automobile, underground
tunnel, and several we cannot discuss here.

00:33:38.159 --> 00:33:43.260
Removal of the blindfold during the transport
process is grounds for immediate termination.

00:33:43.260 --> 00:33:49.070
Personnel assigned to SCP-231-7 must undergo
heavy psychological testing before being cleared

00:33:49.070 --> 00:33:50.970
to enter the site.

00:33:50.970 --> 00:33:56.690
Individuals must score at least 72 points
on the Milgram Obedience Examination, be unmarried,

00:33:56.690 --> 00:34:01.500
have no offspring, and express nothing less
than total loyalty to the Foundation.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:06.240
Normal psychological screening procedures
against Axis II disorders are waived, so long

00:34:06.240 --> 00:34:11.750
as the Class-D personnel in question has the
mental capacity to carry out Procedure 110-Montauk

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:13.419
as needed.

00:34:13.419 --> 00:34:19.220
Personnel who express sympathy towards SCP-231-7's
plight and/or express a desire to rescue or

00:34:19.220 --> 00:34:25.579
sympathize towards SCP-231-7 will be transferred
to another project without delay.

00:34:25.579 --> 00:34:29.399
Any actual rescue attempts will be met with
immediate termination.

00:34:29.399 --> 00:34:34.540
Personnel who have served on the staff of
SCP-231-7's Containment Team are not required

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:37.149
to divulge that information to others.

00:34:37.149 --> 00:34:43.061
No official record shall be kept of the names
of any staff assigned to SCP-231-7, nor will

00:34:43.061 --> 00:34:46.740
said service appear in the personnel files
of said staff.

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:53.300
While on-site, individuals assigned to SCP-231-7
will be issued concealing helmets with integrated

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.659
voice changers to protect their identity.

00:34:55.659 --> 00:35:00.900
On-site staff are not to remove said uniforms
in the presence of other staff members.

00:35:00.900 --> 00:35:04.900
Off-duty hours are to be spent in private
quarters alone.

00:35:04.900 --> 00:35:10.720
Six Class D Personnel are to be assigned to
SCP-231-7 each month for the purposes of carrying

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:12.510
out Procedure 110-Montauk.

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:17.100
Violent criminals are not to be used for this
purpose due to the possibility of accidental

00:35:17.100 --> 00:35:20.670
fatality during the 110-Montauk process."

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:25.541
Dr. Collingwood swallowed the lump of dread
in her throat, her palms beginning to sweat.

00:35:25.541 --> 00:35:32.270
SCP-231-7 was a young woman, retrieved during
a police raid on a warehouse owned by the

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:34.170
Children of the Scarlet King.

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:39.080
She was one of seven women found there, the
first of which went into labor 24 hours after

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:40.320
being rescued.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:43.550
She did not give birth to an ordinary child,
however.

00:35:43.550 --> 00:35:47.750
She gave birth to a monster that took the
lives of dozens of civilians.

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:49.660
She did not survive the birth.

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:53.711
The second woman was lost shortly after, also
succumbing to the dangers of the creature

00:35:53.711 --> 00:35:55.700
waiting to be born from her womb.

00:35:55.700 --> 00:36:00.839
One by one, the survivors, the women chosen
by this cult to become the new brides of the

00:36:00.839 --> 00:36:02.410
Scarlet King, fell.

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:06.250
Soon enough, there was only one left.

00:36:06.250 --> 00:36:11.910
SCP-231-7, kept contained at a Foundation
Site in a hospital bed that she could never

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:12.910
leave.

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:16.829
Dr. Collingwood's stomach churned as horrible
images flooded her mind.

00:36:16.829 --> 00:36:21.810
The notes from attending researchers in SCP-231's
file insisted they were doing all they could

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:27.210
to save the woman, to free her from her fate,
but that there was little they could do.

00:36:27.210 --> 00:36:33.160
Each birth opened a gateway from this world
to another, to a world of darkness and despair,

00:36:33.160 --> 00:36:36.620
and they could not allow the final seal to
be broken.

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:40.710
Simply putting the woman out of her misery
would cause the effect to jump to a new host,

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:42.050
a new bride.

00:36:42.050 --> 00:36:44.109
There was nothing to be done.

00:36:44.109 --> 00:36:48.359
The file fell from Dr. Collingwood's hand
as she pushed herself to her feet, running

00:36:48.359 --> 00:36:49.710
out into the hallway.

00:36:49.710 --> 00:36:51.550
"Who put these on my desk?"

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:54.670
She shouted, her voice echoing in the empty
hall.

00:36:54.670 --> 00:36:55.670
"Well?

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:56.670
What do you want from me?"

00:36:56.670 --> 00:36:57.970
But no answer came.

00:36:57.970 --> 00:37:03.230
Slowly, she returned to her office and sank
down into her chair, haunted by what she had

00:37:03.230 --> 00:37:04.230
read.

00:37:04.230 --> 00:37:07.910
She had seen horrific things in her time at
the Foundation, but nothing that had shaken

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:10.329
her to her core quite like this.

00:37:10.329 --> 00:37:15.260
The silence in Dr. Collingwood's office was
broken by the sound of a tinny beep, coming

00:37:15.260 --> 00:37:16.670
from her computer.

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:22.760
She turned on the screen, and saw the source
of the alert: One New Message, from O5 Command.

00:37:22.760 --> 00:37:24.920
What could they possibly have to say to her?

00:37:24.920 --> 00:37:28.300
Could it have something to do with the files
that had wound up on her desk?

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:33.609
She accepted the message, quickly completed
the mandatory multimodal biometric ID scan,

00:37:33.609 --> 00:37:34.900
and began to read.

00:37:34.900 --> 00:37:40.340
"Hello, Doctor Collingwood, and congratulations
on your new appointment as SCP-999's head

00:37:40.340 --> 00:37:45.310
researcher, one of the cushiest and most enviable
assignments in the entire Foundation.

00:37:45.310 --> 00:37:51.150
SCP-999 is one of the few anomalies in our
custody who will not only never attempt to

00:37:51.150 --> 00:37:55.750
harm you but will actively try to save your
life if you’re ever in any danger.

00:37:55.750 --> 00:38:00.119
Though your initial reaction when receiving
this assignment was no doubt elation, you

00:38:00.119 --> 00:38:05.089
may have thought it was odd that such a seemingly
low-risk position was assigned by the O5 council

00:38:05.089 --> 00:38:06.089
directly.

00:38:06.089 --> 00:38:09.579
If you had already heard rumors of this prior
to your assignment, you probably thought it

00:38:09.579 --> 00:38:14.260
was mere nepotism; the O5s protecting their
friends and loved ones by giving them the

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:15.770
safest job possible."

00:38:15.770 --> 00:38:20.910
Dr. Collingwood sat back in her chair, laughing
to herself and shaking her head.

00:38:20.910 --> 00:38:25.420
She would never admit it, but she absolutely
had heard those rumors, even believed them

00:38:25.420 --> 00:38:27.310
until it had happened to her.

00:38:27.310 --> 00:38:32.030
"Unless you are so narcissistic to think that
someone on the O5 council must be your secret

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:35.890
admirer, you’ve likely realized that this
is not the case.

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:41.630
To understand why this is our concern, you
need to know about 999’s origins.

00:38:41.630 --> 00:38:46.700
You may have noticed that its file makes no
mention of where it was discovered.

00:38:46.700 --> 00:38:48.950
This is a deliberate omission.

00:38:48.950 --> 00:38:52.910
If you’re not familiar with the mythology
of the Scarlet King, I suggest you read up

00:38:52.910 --> 00:38:53.910
on him.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:58.130
There’s plenty of unclassified information
on him in the Foundation database.

00:38:58.130 --> 00:39:02.380
All that’s relevant for now is that he is
- to the best of our knowledge - the most

00:39:02.380 --> 00:39:05.369
powerful malevolent entity in the Multiverse."

00:39:05.369 --> 00:39:08.560
Her stomach dropped at the mention of The
Scarlet King.

00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:11.200
She was right, this was all connected somehow.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:15.859
She leaned forward, eyes darting across the
words with increasing intensity as she read

00:39:15.859 --> 00:39:16.859
on.

00:39:16.859 --> 00:39:20.510
"You've been with us since you were a research
assistant, Dr. Collingwood.

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:25.140
In that time, I assume you've heard many rumors
about some of the horrific things we do here

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:28.770
at the Foundation that you've never personally
witnessed.

00:39:28.770 --> 00:39:32.180
I regret to inform you that these rumors are
true.

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:34.210
Or at least, they were.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:39.330
A thaumaturgical cult calling itself the Children
of the Scarlet King enacted a ritual wherein

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:44.670
seven young girls became effigies for each
of the Scarlet King’s seven brides, allowing

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:46.550
them to bear his horrid offspring.

00:39:46.550 --> 00:39:52.450
How they obtained the knowledge to perform
this ritual is unclear since all we ever recovered

00:39:52.450 --> 00:39:54.160
were handwritten notebooks.

00:39:54.160 --> 00:39:59.940
Superficial resemblances to some Sarkic practices
has led some to speculate that the Children

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:03.849
of the Scarlet King may have some ties to
modern Sarkic cults.

00:40:03.849 --> 00:40:10.470
It's an interesting idea, but no concrete
evidence has ever been found to link the two.

00:40:10.470 --> 00:40:13.180
Investigation into the matter is ongoing.

00:40:13.180 --> 00:40:17.690
As for the ritual itself, each birth caused
more destruction than the last.

00:40:17.690 --> 00:40:22.339
The writings of the cult’s priest predicted
nothing less than the Apocalypse if the Seventh

00:40:22.339 --> 00:40:28.160
Bride gave birth, which could only be prevented
if Procedure 110-Montauk was performed without

00:40:28.160 --> 00:40:30.570
fail each and every day.

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:36.030
To our seemingly great fortune, the notebook
contained detailed instructions on how 110-Montauk

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:37.859
was to be carried out.

00:40:37.859 --> 00:40:42.210
Needless to say, we found this suspiciously
convenient.

00:40:42.210 --> 00:40:46.089
Why would they devise a countermeasure to
prevent the very apocalypse they were trying

00:40:46.089 --> 00:40:47.089
to invoke?

00:40:47.089 --> 00:40:50.140
We needed more information regarding these
entities.

00:40:50.140 --> 00:40:55.950
Fortunately, our archaeologists have unearthed
numerous tablets, scrolls, and artifacts of

00:40:55.950 --> 00:40:57.600
the ancient Daevas.

00:40:57.600 --> 00:41:03.030
They were a sadistic and warmongering people
who were granted unholy power and knowledge

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:08.030
by the Scarlet King as a reward for the death
and suffering they caused.

00:41:08.030 --> 00:41:13.060
One of the Daevite tablets in our possession,
found covered in dust and blood, was a theogony

00:41:13.060 --> 00:41:15.710
for the Scarlet King and his Brides.

00:41:15.710 --> 00:41:17.720
It was quite informative.

00:41:17.720 --> 00:41:22.680
The information that we found most relevant
to our situation was that the Seventh Bride

00:41:22.680 --> 00:41:25.040
was not like her sisters.

00:41:25.040 --> 00:41:29.099
She was never completely broken by her King’s
subjugation.

00:41:29.099 --> 00:41:33.950
Instead of monsters, she gave birth to great
heroes in the hopes that they would destroy

00:41:33.950 --> 00:41:37.440
her sisters’ children and overthrow their
father.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:44.069
Thus far, all have failed, but by a vote of
7 in favor, 6 against - admittedly more out

00:41:44.069 --> 00:41:49.510
of concern for Procedure 110-Montauk's lack
of viability as a long-term containment strategy

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:54.380
than out of empathy for the girl - the O5
council decided to believe that the Seventh

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:59.619
Bride still remained unbroken and that her
child would be an asset to us.

00:41:59.619 --> 00:42:06.000
At the risk of causing an XK-Class End of
the World Scenario, SCP-231-7 was relieved

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:13.720
from Procedure 110-Montauk following the deaths
of SCP-231-1 through -6, and was allowed to

00:42:13.720 --> 00:42:15.830
give birth.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:18.040
SCP-999 was the result."

00:42:18.040 --> 00:42:20.492
Dr. Collingwood gasped, her hand flying to
her mouth.

00:42:20.492 --> 00:42:25.490
A Research Assistant walking by her office
paused, looking at her curiously through the

00:42:25.490 --> 00:42:26.490
window.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:29.740
She waved them off with a forced smile, and
turned back to the screen.

00:42:29.740 --> 00:42:32.369
Surely she couldn't have read that correctly?

00:42:32.369 --> 00:42:35.270
But, no, there it was, plain as can be:

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:37.330
"Go ahead and read that again.

00:42:37.330 --> 00:42:42.940
Be sure you understand it in all its preposterous
ridiculousness; The Tickle Monster is the

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:44.920
child of the Scarlet King.

00:42:44.920 --> 00:42:49.109
We’ve been running a counterintelligence
campaign ever since, which is why everyone

00:42:49.109 --> 00:42:53.510
and their mother thinks we’ve still got
a girl strapped to a rack in a bunker somewhere.

00:42:53.510 --> 00:42:55.550
Let them think that.

00:42:55.550 --> 00:42:59.240
Far better for everyone that the Children
of the Scarlet King believe that they’re

00:42:59.240 --> 00:43:04.130
playing us for fools than for them to know
that there is a threat to their King.

00:43:04.130 --> 00:43:06.650
The girl herself is fine, by the way.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:12.400
She was cured of the trauma from her ordeal
by SCP-999, at which point it was decided

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:18.280
she could be returned to her family so long
as they were all given class F amnestics,

00:43:18.280 --> 00:43:24.080
implanted with new identities, and relocated
to a town at least 1000 km away from the Children

00:43:24.080 --> 00:43:27.140
of the Scarlet King's nearest known activity.

00:43:27.140 --> 00:43:31.940
On the insistence of the Ethics Committee,
the family was also given a seven-figure payout

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:37.490
as compensation for our repeated…misdeeds
against their daughter, as were the families

00:43:37.490 --> 00:43:39.280
of the other SCP-231s.

00:43:39.280 --> 00:43:43.450
I suppose it was technically malpractice on
our part.

00:43:43.450 --> 00:43:47.630
In case we have any moles for the Children
of the Scarlet King in the Foundation, as

00:43:47.630 --> 00:43:53.580
far as anyone else knows 231-7's family were
killed in front of her as part of 110-Montauk.

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:55.900
I’m sure you’re skeptical.

00:43:55.900 --> 00:43:57.240
Are we insane?

00:43:57.240 --> 00:44:01.690
How could our sweet little tickle monster
ever hope to dethrone a Lovecraftian Horror

00:44:01.690 --> 00:44:03.569
of unparalleled might?

00:44:03.569 --> 00:44:06.880
Well, SCP-999 is less than a decade old.

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:11.540
It’s still just a child, and nowhere near
its full strength.

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:13.960
Even so, its power is incredible.

00:44:13.960 --> 00:44:20.220
Even brief interaction with SCP-999 can permanently
cure severe depression and PTSD, and more

00:44:20.220 --> 00:44:25.720
recent experiments have resulted in the complete
reformation of D-class personnel who were

00:44:25.720 --> 00:44:28.700
previously unrepentant sociopaths.

00:44:28.700 --> 00:44:33.850
This effect is not chemical, but psychic,
and one day it may grow powerful enough that

00:44:33.850 --> 00:44:37.630
not even the Scarlet King himself will be
immune.

00:44:37.630 --> 00:44:40.670
The experiment with SCP-682 was most remarkable.

00:44:40.670 --> 00:44:47.010
Based on multiple Daevite texts, including
descriptions from SCP-140 itself, we are reasonably

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:51.369
certain that 682 is the offspring of the Fourth
Scarlet Bride.

00:44:51.369 --> 00:44:57.660
If this is true, then SCP-999 is already strong
enough to temporarily quell the malevolence

00:44:57.660 --> 00:45:00.060
of its own eldritch siblings.

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:05.630
One day 999 could very well be strong enough
to permanently reform its family members just

00:45:05.630 --> 00:45:07.680
as it reforms human beings.

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:13.349
It will not overthrow the Scarlet King by
force, but with light and love and laughter

00:45:13.349 --> 00:45:15.420
that can brighten the blackest of hearts.

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:19.930
999 is not, in reality, a safe class SCP.

00:45:19.930 --> 00:45:21.380
It is Thaumiel.

00:45:21.380 --> 00:45:24.940
It is the best and really the only weapon
we have against some of the most powerful

00:45:24.940 --> 00:45:27.710
hostile entities known to exist.

00:45:27.710 --> 00:45:32.980
By all means, doctor, enjoy the relative safety
of your new position, but do keep in mind

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:37.170
that SCP-999 is not a mere pet that we fancy.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:41.760
It is one of our most valuable assets and
must be safeguarded at all costs.

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:46.550
Its safety and well-being are paramount, and
you are not at liberty to share this information

00:45:46.550 --> 00:45:50.890
to anyone without level 5/999 security clearance.

00:45:50.890 --> 00:45:55.220
As per protocol, unauthorized disclosure of
level 5 classified information will result

00:45:55.220 --> 00:45:56.990
in your termination.

00:45:56.990 --> 00:46:01.120
This e-mail will automatically delete as soon
as you leave the terminal, so feel free to

00:46:01.120 --> 00:46:06.200
reread it as many times as necessary to remember
all of the pertinent information.

00:46:06.200 --> 00:46:08.869
Take good care of our little tickle monster.

00:46:08.869 --> 00:46:11.749
The fate of the Multiverse may well depend
on it.

00:46:11.749 --> 00:46:16.230
- Your secret admirer (if anyone asks), O5-1."

00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:21.150
Dr. Collingwood let out a long, slow breath
as her mind did its best to process everything

00:46:21.150 --> 00:46:22.450
she just read.

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:26.650
The message looked official, and, when she
really thought about it...this all made a

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:28.870
twisted kind of sense.

00:46:28.870 --> 00:46:35.079
What could SCP-999 possibly be, if not some
sort of mythic hero of divine prophecy?

00:46:35.079 --> 00:46:39.470
What other explanation could there be for
something so utterly, perfectly good?

00:46:39.470 --> 00:46:45.410
Maybe one day that happy orange slime would
save them all, and do it with a smile.

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:48.380
But for now, it was just a little, innocent
child.

00:46:48.380 --> 00:46:52.790
No need to burden it with talk of heroism,
of epic battles between good and evil.

00:46:52.790 --> 00:46:59.609
For now, she would just pour SCP-999 a heaping
bowl of M&amp;Ms, give it a gentle pat, and say

00:46:59.609 --> 00:47:00.609
"thank you."

00:47:00.609 --> 00:47:05.589
The tickle monster gurgled excitedly in response,
nuzzling his new head researcher's hand.

00:47:05.589 --> 00:47:10.990
He didn't understand what she was thanking
him for, but he appreciated it all the same.

00:47:10.990 --> 00:47:15.740
SCP-106 is like something out of a night terror.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:21.550
That gray, rotting flesh, those wide, empty
eyes like those of a shark, the relentless

00:47:21.550 --> 00:47:25.300
corrosion and decay that follows him everywhere.

00:47:25.300 --> 00:47:31.290
But what if I told you that, beneath that
horrifying exterior, was a heartbreaking,

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:32.930
tragic backstory?

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:38.700
Some of our more eagle-eyed viewers know that
SCP-106 has a wide variety of potential origin

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:44.390
stories, different events that may have led
to the Old Man we all know and fear today.

00:47:44.390 --> 00:47:48.700
So, this is just one of several hypothetical
backstories.

00:47:48.700 --> 00:47:53.260
With that little caveat out of the way, it's
time to see if we can bring a tear to your

00:47:53.260 --> 00:47:58.510
eye with the tragic tale of SCP-106.

00:47:58.510 --> 00:48:04.430
Back in the year 2000, Dr. Robert Scranton
and his wife, Dr. Anna Lang, were Head Researchers

00:48:04.430 --> 00:48:06.030
at Site-120.

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:12.330
The two of them shared a lot of things: a
home, a life, a shared love of Bon Jovi songs,

00:48:12.330 --> 00:48:17.980
but one of the things they had in common was
a passion for experimental research.

00:48:17.980 --> 00:48:22.670
The two worked together to develop a device
called the "Lang-Scranton Stabilizer", which

00:48:22.670 --> 00:48:28.569
was an early prototype of the Reality Anchors
used by the SCP Foundation to this day.

00:48:28.569 --> 00:48:32.630
During a round of routine tests, an earthquake
unexpectedly struck Site-120.

00:48:32.630 --> 00:48:38.770
Dr. Scranton was working in Reality Lab A
when the ground beneath him began to rumble.

00:48:38.770 --> 00:48:44.290
The LSS shook from the activity, components
sliding out of place or breaking off altogether.

00:48:44.290 --> 00:48:49.619
By the time guards, and Dr. Lang, arrived
to inspect the scene, both Dr. Scranton and

00:48:49.619 --> 00:48:52.650
the LSS Control Panel were gone.

00:48:52.650 --> 00:48:56.390
Upon his disappearance, Dr. Scranton was initially
presumed dead.

00:48:56.390 --> 00:49:02.200
However, five years, eleven months, and 21
days later, his whereabouts became clear.

00:49:02.200 --> 00:49:10.099
On December 23, 2005, the LSS Control Panel
reappeared in Site-120's Reality Lab A. It

00:49:10.099 --> 00:49:15.750
was covered in unknown organic matter, and
reeked of vomit, blood, and death.

00:49:15.750 --> 00:49:21.000
As Dr. Anna Lang waded into the area, against
the warnings of her colleagues, she was overwhelmed

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:22.599
with grief and horror.

00:49:22.599 --> 00:49:27.090
She choked back tears as she accessed the
control panel, retrieving the audio files

00:49:27.090 --> 00:49:32.310
left there, and realized that Dr. Scranton
had been keeping a log of his time in whatever

00:49:32.310 --> 00:49:34.869
place the disaster had sent him.

00:49:34.869 --> 00:49:40.580
The husband she had lost had, in a way, come
back to her, but she could not see him.

00:49:40.580 --> 00:49:43.720
She could hear his voice, but where was he?

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:47.420
There was so much blood, so much unidentified
organic matter.

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:52.069
It looked like something had exploded onto
the control panel, and she didn't want to

00:49:52.069 --> 00:49:55.430
think about what, or who, that could have
been.

00:49:55.430 --> 00:49:59.710
As Robert's wedding ring fell to the floor,
landing in the muck, the emotional strain

00:49:59.710 --> 00:50:02.020
became too much for Dr. Lang.

00:50:02.020 --> 00:50:05.089
She collapsed onto the ground, hitting her
head on the pavement.

00:50:05.089 --> 00:50:10.299
Dr. Lang survived her fall, left only with
a tender spot on her skull, and a wealth of

00:50:10.299 --> 00:50:13.470
questions she was almost too afraid to answer.

00:50:13.470 --> 00:50:18.920
But she had to find out, with answers at her
fingertips, she had to know what had become

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:20.410
of her dear Robert.

00:50:20.410 --> 00:50:25.530
So, with the help of the other Site-120 Researchers,
who promised to step in when it became too

00:50:25.530 --> 00:50:31.020
painful, Dr. Lang began to go through the
audio archives left behind by her lost love

00:50:31.020 --> 00:50:33.309
in the years since he had vanished.

00:50:33.309 --> 00:50:36.319
For the first eight days, Dr. Scranton didn't
speak.

00:50:36.319 --> 00:50:39.150
At least, not in any recognizable words.

00:50:39.150 --> 00:50:44.700
He hyperventilated, he muttered to himself,
he sobbed, he screamed with pure rage and

00:50:44.700 --> 00:50:47.420
despair, but he didn't say a word.

00:50:47.420 --> 00:50:51.730
He paced around the new space, clawing for
a way out, throwing his shoulder against any

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:57.079
hard surface he could in an attempt to bust
open a door, but he was trapped.

00:50:57.079 --> 00:51:02.061
On his eleventh day in the pocket dimension
outside of any world he knew, Dr. Scranton

00:51:02.061 --> 00:51:03.390
began to speak.

00:51:03.390 --> 00:51:09.890
He reminded himself, reminded anyone who might
some day listen, who he was: Robert Scranton.

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:11.309
Age 39.

00:51:11.309 --> 00:51:14.170
Born September 19, 1961.

00:51:14.170 --> 00:51:16.609
Favorite color, blue.

00:51:16.609 --> 00:51:19.410
Favorite song, "Living on a Prayer."

00:51:19.410 --> 00:51:20.760
Wife, Anna.

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:25.760
Every day, he repeated these simple truths,
seeking comfort in his memories of Anna and

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:27.549
the life they once shared.

00:51:27.549 --> 00:51:32.241
Her green eyes, their wedding day, her dressed
in white shoving a piece of cake into his

00:51:32.241 --> 00:51:35.980
face with a giddy laugh and a sparkle in her
eye.

00:51:35.980 --> 00:51:40.750
He hoped that she was okay, that she had managed
to escape the chaos with her life, that she

00:51:40.750 --> 00:51:42.900
had been spared a similar fate.

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:48.830
He repeated her nickname, the password he
had set for the control panel: "Anna bo banna."

00:51:48.830 --> 00:51:51.780
At this point, he realized that the machine
was still recording.

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:55.130
He called out for help, desperate to see if
anyone could hear him.

00:51:55.130 --> 00:51:56.600
"Is anyone there?!

00:51:56.600 --> 00:51:57.600
Hello?

00:51:57.600 --> 00:51:58.600
Anyone?

00:51:58.600 --> 00:51:59.600
ANYONE?!"

00:51:59.600 --> 00:52:00.850
But of course, there was no answer.

00:52:00.850 --> 00:52:04.740
"Why the hell is this thing even working,
it can't be working."

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:05.740
He mused.

00:52:05.740 --> 00:52:06.849
"What the hell?!"

00:52:06.849 --> 00:52:11.250
He couldn't see anything, not the switches
or the knobs, only the blinking of the red

00:52:11.250 --> 00:52:12.920
"recording" light.

00:52:12.920 --> 00:52:17.260
He was growing hungrier and thirstier by the
day, and though he knew he should be dead

00:52:17.260 --> 00:52:21.140
from dehydration by now, somehow he wasn't.

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:22.589
He was still alive.

00:52:22.589 --> 00:52:27.369
Soon, he found the controls, and determined
he had been trapped in this place for two

00:52:27.369 --> 00:52:31.369
weeks, three days, forty-seven hours, and
fifty-eight minutes.

00:52:31.369 --> 00:52:36.230
He realized at this point that, no matter
how hungry he got, no matter how long he went

00:52:36.230 --> 00:52:39.020
without water, he would not die.

00:52:39.020 --> 00:52:44.670
Somehow, in this place, his body kept going,
his heart ticked along, far past what should

00:52:44.670 --> 00:52:46.720
have been his expiration date.

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:50.990
He retrieved a small picture of Anna from
his pocket, gazing at her face by the faint

00:52:50.990 --> 00:52:52.119
glow of the red light.

00:52:52.119 --> 00:52:57.390
Two months, four days, and three hours in,
he recorded a formal message:

00:52:57.390 --> 00:52:59.540
"My name is…

00:52:59.540 --> 00:53:00.540
Robert Scranton.

00:53:00.540 --> 00:53:03.550
I am a former Head Researcher of Site…

00:53:03.550 --> 00:53:10.110
120, a Foundation facility dedicated to studying
various reality-bending SCPs, for the purpose

00:53:10.110 --> 00:53:13.460
of developing more advanced countermeasures
towards such threats.

00:53:13.460 --> 00:53:19.150
For the last… red light, speak to me,
'Two months, eight days, sixteen hours'

00:53:19.150 --> 00:53:20.750
What red light said.

00:53:20.750 --> 00:53:24.350
I have been trapped in what I believe to be
an empty pocket dimension.

00:53:24.350 --> 00:53:25.350
Alone.

00:53:25.350 --> 00:53:26.790
Yeah… alone.

00:53:26.790 --> 00:53:27.790
All alone.

00:53:27.790 --> 00:53:29.690
I'm calling this place SCP…

00:53:29.690 --> 00:53:33.970
I don't know, I can't remember where we are,
screw it.

00:53:33.970 --> 00:53:36.590
I don't know what's happened in the past…

00:53:36.590 --> 00:53:38.049
red light, please, again.

00:53:38.049 --> 00:53:40.730
Two months, eight days, sixteen hours.

00:53:40.730 --> 00:53:41.730
But…

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:45.130
no one else is around to argue, and at this
point…

00:53:45.130 --> 00:53:48.930
I'm just talking into this control panel to
keep myself together.

00:53:48.930 --> 00:53:49.930
I…

00:53:49.930 --> 00:53:51.010
I need to keep a record.

00:53:51.010 --> 00:53:55.890
There might be some poor chump in the future
who ends up like me, and… if this ever actually

00:53:55.890 --> 00:53:59.720
makes it out… maybe, maybe I can help stop
that from happening.

00:53:59.720 --> 00:54:06.030
That's all I have going for me right now,
and I really need something to go for, hahahaha…

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:08.329
…So, yeah, Robert…

00:54:08.329 --> 00:54:09.329
Scranton…

00:54:09.329 --> 00:54:13.079
documenting a new SCP for… future research
purposes.

00:54:13.079 --> 00:54:14.380
That'll have to do.

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:15.839
Here we go!"

00:54:15.839 --> 00:54:19.000
A few days later, he added to his informal
SCP log:

00:54:19.000 --> 00:54:22.880
"I don't know if we could… contain wherever
I am.

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:25.020
It's… definitely not on Earth.

00:54:25.020 --> 00:54:26.830
To be honest I don't know where it is.

00:54:26.830 --> 00:54:27.830
I…

00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:30.490
I think it has do something with the Stabilizer
prototype…

00:54:30.490 --> 00:54:32.640
I'll explain that more later.

00:54:32.640 --> 00:54:37.320
Okay… um… yeah, wherever I am, I don't
think it can be contained much as… created.

00:54:37.320 --> 00:54:41.430
No, no, that's not the word I'm looking for.

00:54:41.430 --> 00:54:42.869
Um… entered.

00:54:42.869 --> 00:54:44.410
Yeah, entered is better.

00:54:44.410 --> 00:54:49.619
I came into this place because of some really
bad reality-bending accident and… no, no,

00:54:49.619 --> 00:54:55.340
Robert, don't be like that yet, you don't
know if there's no exit yet.

00:54:55.340 --> 00:54:56.340
This place…

00:54:56.340 --> 00:54:59.900
It's some sort of reality gap, I think.

00:54:59.900 --> 00:55:01.050
It's dark.

00:55:01.050 --> 00:55:02.050
Really dark.

00:55:02.050 --> 00:55:05.809
As in, this little red light that shows my
words are actually being recorded is the only

00:55:05.809 --> 00:55:08.260
visible light in this entire place.

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:11.720
I can't see my hands, and I can barely see
the control panel here.

00:55:11.720 --> 00:55:16.609
I've had to basically use the light as a center,
and remember how many steps I take and in

00:55:16.609 --> 00:55:17.609
which direction.

00:55:17.609 --> 00:55:19.299
I haven't gone past a hundred yet.

00:55:19.299 --> 00:55:20.299
I'm too…

00:55:20.299 --> 00:55:21.930
I'm too scared to.

00:55:21.930 --> 00:55:22.930
Heh.

00:55:22.930 --> 00:55:25.210
I wonder if my hair is turning white, right
now?

00:55:25.210 --> 00:55:27.540
I can't even see what color it is anymore.

00:55:27.540 --> 00:55:30.559
Speaking of which, my head has been a bit
itchy recently.

00:55:30.559 --> 00:55:36.039
If I don't concentrate on it, it's fine, but
I feel this… tingling all over my face.

00:55:36.039 --> 00:55:37.680
I'm not sure why.

00:55:37.680 --> 00:55:39.710
I… just discovered a new property of this
place.

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:44.490
All this time, I've been thinking I might
be walking on… some sort of… flat ground,

00:55:44.490 --> 00:55:45.490
if you will.

00:55:45.490 --> 00:55:49.400
I kept eye contact with little red as far
as I could see, and it seems I could walk

00:55:49.400 --> 00:55:50.880
in a straight, flat path.

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:55.680
Jesus, my head is buzzing right now, I think
the adrenaline is still kicking…

00:55:55.680 --> 00:56:01.970
But, if my hypothesis is correct, and this
really is some sort of reality… void, then

00:56:01.970 --> 00:56:04.190
there shouldn't be anything to walk on.

00:56:04.190 --> 00:56:07.460
Now that I think about, the whole time I've
been in here, it's felt like…

00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:11.859
I'm walking, but I'm also swimming through
something.

00:56:11.859 --> 00:56:15.040
It's like I'm walking through really thick
black gel.

00:56:15.040 --> 00:56:21.220
There's enough tension to keep me on a…
"surface", but if I… imagine myself pressing

00:56:21.220 --> 00:56:22.799
down hard enough, I can descend.

00:56:22.799 --> 00:56:23.799
Wait.

00:56:23.799 --> 00:56:25.859
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, I think…

00:56:25.859 --> 00:56:28.420
I think I need to test this more, I'll be
back.

00:56:28.420 --> 00:56:30.549
Two months, seventeen days, two hours.

00:56:30.549 --> 00:56:35.820
Navigation is largely affected by… conscious
impulses to travel in a certain direction.

00:56:35.820 --> 00:56:41.420
So, this definitely isn't a complete reality
gap, at least according to mine and Anna's

00:56:41.420 --> 00:56:42.420
theories.

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:47.660
If-if it were I wouldn't have been able to
move at all, since space wouldn't have existed.

00:56:47.660 --> 00:56:52.520
Holy shit, okay, okay, this makes a lot more
sense than it did before, great, great job,

00:56:52.520 --> 00:56:53.609
Robert, you're getting there.

00:56:53.609 --> 00:56:58.270
…Come to think of it, I should've realized
that sooner when I was able to move in a flat

00:56:58.270 --> 00:57:00.579
plane to and from little red.

00:57:00.579 --> 00:57:06.350
It also explains why I'm not dead from dehydration
or hunger yet, time barely passes in here.

00:57:06.350 --> 00:57:10.789
Okay yeah, so, I stood right next to little
red, and went straight…

00:57:10.789 --> 00:57:11.789
"down."

00:57:11.789 --> 00:57:15.240
Okay, from here on out, imagine little red
as the origin of a 3D space.

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:16.619
I went straight…

00:57:16.619 --> 00:57:21.349
down, right, yeah, and then… and then I
was then able to come back "up" to little

00:57:21.349 --> 00:57:22.349
red again.

00:57:22.349 --> 00:57:25.039
I've also been able to "fly" above red.

00:57:25.039 --> 00:57:29.810
Movement in here is slow, like I said, gel
analogy, best I can describe it by."

00:57:29.810 --> 00:57:35.690
As Robert became more familiar with his surroundings,
with the truth of where he found himself,

00:57:35.690 --> 00:57:40.839
something became clear: Of all the things
in this new reality, he and his little red

00:57:40.839 --> 00:57:42.380
light were the realest.

00:57:42.380 --> 00:57:46.039
With time, they would begin to degrade, to
fall apart.

00:57:46.039 --> 00:57:49.750
He had, in estimation, three years before
he would be gone for good.

00:57:49.750 --> 00:57:53.500
He had to try and look for an end to this
place, a way out.

00:57:53.500 --> 00:57:55.059
What did he have to lose?

00:57:55.059 --> 00:57:57.950
As it turned out, what he had to lose was
himself.

00:57:57.950 --> 00:58:01.950
He couldn't feel his legs anymore, couldn't
keep a grip on his own body.

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:07.630
He ran in one straight line for six months,
but found no edge, no end in sight.

00:58:07.630 --> 00:58:11.390
He went down for eight months straight, and
never hit bottom.

00:58:11.390 --> 00:58:16.289
His sole companion was the little red light,
and he continued to talk to it as if it could

00:58:16.289 --> 00:58:17.540
ever answer.

00:58:17.540 --> 00:58:20.480
His hands began to phase into each other,
his thumb came off without any pain, the integrity

00:58:20.480 --> 00:58:22.770
of his form beginning to fade and warp.

00:58:22.770 --> 00:58:26.109
No matter what happened to him, he could not
die.

00:58:26.109 --> 00:58:32.339
His sanity began to crumble, his voice breaking
into uncontrollable sobs and hysterical laughter.

00:58:32.339 --> 00:58:35.440
Whoever Robert Scranton had been, he was gone.

00:58:35.440 --> 00:58:40.349
He was something else now, and who knew how
long that new thing would last?

00:58:40.349 --> 00:58:44.859
For the next twenty-five years, Dr. Anna Lang
tried her best to put the thought of her lost

00:58:44.859 --> 00:58:46.220
husband out of her head.

00:58:46.220 --> 00:58:51.079
It almost worked some days, she almost forgot
that hole in her heart.

00:58:51.079 --> 00:58:56.430
Then, one fateful day, Site-120 received a
deadly visitor.

00:58:56.430 --> 00:59:02.319
SCP-106 reached through the back wall of her
lab, dripping its horrible rot all over the

00:59:02.319 --> 00:59:03.319
floor.

00:59:03.319 --> 00:59:07.990
It grabbed for her, its touch melting her
lab coat as she tore away from its toothless

00:59:07.990 --> 00:59:11.500
grin, the singular focus in its milky eyes.

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:16.300
The old man chased her down, pushing past
a guard's bullets, tearing through everything

00:59:16.300 --> 00:59:17.540
in its path.

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.390
She had done enough work on dimensional anomalies
to recognize one when she saw it, and this

00:59:22.390 --> 00:59:26.119
was one of the most destructive she had ever
encountered.

00:59:26.119 --> 00:59:29.720
When it finally caught her, it said one word:
"Red."

00:59:29.720 --> 00:59:33.780
Then, another, one that made her eyes fill
with tears.

00:59:33.780 --> 00:59:34.780
"Anna..."

00:59:34.780 --> 00:59:40.010
Dr. Robert Scranton had found a way to escape
the dead-end prison he had fallen into, that

00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:42.100
gap between the worlds.

00:59:42.100 --> 00:59:47.049
All it had taken was giving up his humanity,
letting that world rearrange his atoms and

00:59:47.049 --> 00:59:51.590
make him something monstrous, something that
destroyed all it touched.

00:59:51.590 --> 00:59:56.010
He couldn't remember why he needed to find
Anna, how he had been separated from her for

00:59:56.010 --> 00:59:59.849
so long, he could only remember that he wanted
her.

00:59:59.849 --> 01:00:01.329
Wanted to be with her.

01:00:01.329 --> 01:00:06.460
Wanted to touch her again, even as she screamed
and melted in his hands.

01:00:06.460 --> 01:00:11.230
SCP-106 once was a man, just like any other
man.

01:00:11.230 --> 01:00:16.470
What he is now shares the vague shape of the
human he used to be, the vaguest hints of

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:20.130
his memories, but that is where the similarities
end.

01:00:20.130 --> 01:00:24.809
Like the rotting flesh of his body might suggest,
he is little more than a shambling corpse

01:00:24.809 --> 01:00:27.180
with a ravenous appetite for violence.

01:00:27.180 --> 01:00:32.690
Dr. Robert Scranton is dead, but he will never
rest.

01:00:32.690 --> 01:00:37.420
Anyone who holds more than just a passing
knowledge of the SCP Foundation has likely

01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:40.990
heard the name of one Doctor Jack Bright.

01:00:40.990 --> 01:00:45.430
Like a lot of the other high-ranking research
staff, his name carries with it a reputation

01:00:45.430 --> 01:00:50.270
for anomalous expertise far beyond many working
at the Foundation.

01:00:50.270 --> 01:00:54.440
Having risen through the ranks and achieved
the position of Personnel Director for Sites

01:00:54.440 --> 01:01:01.700
17, 19, and 63, Doctor Bright may just seem
like a highly competent and devoted member

01:01:01.700 --> 01:01:03.530
of the organization.

01:01:03.530 --> 01:01:09.210
But the history of the man himself is one
that will be forever linked with tragedy and

01:01:09.210 --> 01:01:10.260
trauma.

01:01:10.260 --> 01:01:15.410
Jack Bright’s life has been turbulent, to
say the least, with themes of rebirth and

01:01:15.410 --> 01:01:21.220
regeneration constantly following him and
his family… and all the curses that those

01:01:21.220 --> 01:01:22.619
ideas bring.

01:01:22.619 --> 01:01:28.780
Born to Adam and Evelyn Bright, young Jack
has sadly never experienced what many would

01:01:28.780 --> 01:01:31.010
consider an ‘ordinary’ life.

01:01:31.010 --> 01:01:37.670
You see, both his parents were also researchers
within the SCP Foundation, meaning that since

01:01:37.670 --> 01:01:42.569
birth, Jack Bright has never known a life
that wasn’t linked with the Foundation and

01:01:42.569 --> 01:01:45.220
its many anomalous happenings.

01:01:45.220 --> 01:01:47.710
And it didn’t stop there either.

01:01:47.710 --> 01:01:52.000
At some point, Doctor Adam Bright would rise
through the ranks of the Foundation, much

01:01:52.000 --> 01:01:54.690
like his son would later come to do.

01:01:54.690 --> 01:01:59.910
Adam progressed from researcher to personnel
director to site director and then eventually

01:01:59.910 --> 01:02:04.079
even to the high station of O5 Council member.

01:02:04.079 --> 01:02:08.470
But this career trajectory within the Foundation
was cut short when Jack Bright’s father

01:02:08.470 --> 01:02:15.359
met an untimely end, assassinated on the orders
of his fellow O5 Council members.

01:02:15.359 --> 01:02:16.400
But why?

01:02:16.400 --> 01:02:20.680
What could have led to young Jack suffering
the loss of his Foundation employed father?

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:23.680
Well, it had to do with the other Bright children.

01:02:23.680 --> 01:02:29.820
You see, Jack was far from an only child;
in fact, he had a considerable number of siblings.

01:02:29.820 --> 01:02:35.289
While you might be forgiven for thinking this
would provide a younger Jack Bright with equals,

01:02:35.289 --> 01:02:39.700
friends that he would at least endure his
anomalous adjacent childhood with, this was

01:02:39.700 --> 01:02:41.960
sadly not the case.

01:02:41.960 --> 01:02:47.089
One of Jack’s brothers, TJ Bright, was born
with his very own anomalous abilities.

01:02:47.089 --> 01:02:52.890
TJ had something of a gift for healing, possessing
the power to absorb the injuries and illnesses

01:02:52.890 --> 01:02:57.049
of anyone around him, purely through physical
touch.

01:02:57.049 --> 01:03:02.279
But the unfortunate side effect is that TJ
also takes on another person’s ailments

01:03:02.279 --> 01:03:07.320
himself, experiencing their pain and sickness,
so they don’t have to.

01:03:07.320 --> 01:03:11.359
If you’re Jack Bright, it had to have been
hard to see your younger brother suffering

01:03:11.359 --> 01:03:16.010
from wounds and diseases passed onto him so
that others don’t suffer.

01:03:16.010 --> 01:03:21.559
But there’s still the lingering question:
what happened to jack and TJ’s father, Adam?

01:03:21.559 --> 01:03:26.930
What led to his assassination, and what role
did TJ play in the events leading up to that

01:03:26.930 --> 01:03:28.319
untimely death?

01:03:28.319 --> 01:03:33.500
Well, like much of Doctor Bright’s family
life, it began with a tragedy.

01:03:33.500 --> 01:03:38.831
Trying for another baby, Adam and Evelyn eventually
had a new daughter, giving Jack, TJ, and their

01:03:38.831 --> 01:03:41.079
other siblings a new sister.

01:03:41.079 --> 01:03:46.830
However, this was sadly short-lived, as the
newest member of the Bright family was stillborn.

01:03:46.830 --> 01:03:51.550
Bearing witness to the grief that would have
caused his parents was no doubt hard on Jack.

01:03:51.550 --> 01:03:54.569
But even more upsetting was what came next.

01:03:54.569 --> 01:04:00.119
Naturally, having both studied anomalies within
the SCP Foundation, Evelyn and Adam were willing

01:04:00.119 --> 01:04:04.119
to defy death itself in order to get their
newly born daughter back.

01:04:04.119 --> 01:04:09.869
And luckily for them, they had been presented
with the perfect anomaly to achieve this goal,

01:04:09.869 --> 01:04:14.849
in the form of their young son TJ and his
regenerative powers.

01:04:14.849 --> 01:04:19.900
However, what resulted, was a creature that
later became known as SCP-321.

01:04:19.900 --> 01:04:26.420
While TJ was able to revive the child, her
body aged at a rapidly accelerated rate.

01:04:26.420 --> 01:04:31.140
She became devoid of melanin in her hair,
eyes, and skin.

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:37.710
Still able to vocalize but unable to intelligibly
speak, SCP-321 is known to have spatial awareness

01:04:37.710 --> 01:04:41.339
issues, as well as a low level of intelligence.

01:04:41.339 --> 01:04:46.690
On top of that, SCP-321 seemed to have inherited
some of her brother TJ Bright’s healing

01:04:46.690 --> 01:04:52.990
abilities, capable of regenerating any injury
she suffers at five times the normal speed,

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:55.730
attributed to an overproduction of stem cells.

01:04:55.730 --> 01:05:02.460
It was this that also caused her body to rapidly
age and grow, with no signs of stopping.

01:05:02.460 --> 01:05:07.619
Taken in by the SCP Foundation and given a
designation – along with TJ, who became

01:05:07.619 --> 01:05:14.120
known as SCP-590 – SCP-321 was separated
from the rest of the Bright family.

01:05:14.120 --> 01:05:18.760
Adam, still a Junior Researcher at the time,
pleaded with his Foundation superiors for

01:05:18.760 --> 01:05:22.200
his daughter to be released back into the
care of her family.

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:25.010
Every time he made the request, it was denied.

01:05:25.010 --> 01:05:29.819
It didn’t seem to matter how much higher
up the managerial ladder of the SCP Foundation

01:05:29.819 --> 01:05:35.730
Adam Bright climbed, there always seemed to
be a message from above telling him ‘no’.

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:38.369
In time, he was appointed to the O5 Council.

01:05:38.369 --> 01:05:44.180
It was then that Adam Bright issued a mandate
for SCP-321’s immediate release… and the

01:05:44.180 --> 01:05:47.520
rest of the Council didn’t take kindly to
that.

01:05:47.520 --> 01:05:52.170
Trying to get his daughter back cost Adam
his life, and meant that young Jack Bright

01:05:52.170 --> 01:05:57.310
had lost not only one of his brothers and
a sister, but his father too.

01:05:57.310 --> 01:06:03.300
Jack Bright assisted the SCP Foundation with
keeping his brother TJ, or SCP-590, contained

01:06:03.300 --> 01:06:04.830
for his own safety.

01:06:04.830 --> 01:06:09.359
Unlike his remaining siblings, Jack still
seemed eager to follow in the footsteps of

01:06:09.359 --> 01:06:10.950
his mom and dad.

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:15.700
Another of his sisters, Claire Pierce, was
angered by the Foundation’s actions and

01:06:15.700 --> 01:06:18.200
how they had treated her family.

01:06:18.200 --> 01:06:22.299
This caused her to join up with the Serpent’s
Hand, a Group of interest with the goal of

01:06:22.299 --> 01:06:26.369
one day exposing the SCP Foundation to the
world.

01:06:26.369 --> 01:06:30.619
Then there was Mikell Bright, who had the
opposite reaction to his sister.

01:06:30.619 --> 01:06:35.390
He devoted himself to the Foundation, becoming
one of their most skilled and highly efficient

01:06:35.390 --> 01:06:39.270
field operatives, known to some as Agent Cowboy.

01:06:39.270 --> 01:06:43.829
Needless to say, there remains plenty of division
between the Bright siblings, something that

01:06:43.829 --> 01:06:50.119
would undoubtedly exact a heavy toll on Jack,
who, for his part, continued his family’s

01:06:50.119 --> 01:06:53.850
legacy by joining the Foundation as a Junior
Researcher.

01:06:53.850 --> 01:06:58.059
This is where his life would once again be
altered forever…

01:06:58.059 --> 01:07:03.599
To begin with, Jack seemed to have it easier
than most starting out at the SCP Foundation.

01:07:03.599 --> 01:07:08.609
As previously mentioned, the Bright name already
carried significant pull within the organization,

01:07:08.609 --> 01:07:12.790
not only allowing him to get his foot through
the proverbial door, but also giving Jack

01:07:12.790 --> 01:07:16.599
a much-needed win after his past family troubles.

01:07:16.599 --> 01:07:21.000
After all, he had a considerable amount of
emotional baggage and trauma to deal with,

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:25.910
so the up-and-coming Junior Researcher perhaps
deserved to coast by for a while.

01:07:25.910 --> 01:07:31.109
Although it wouldn’t last long, and Jack
Bright would be irreversibly and irreparably

01:07:31.109 --> 01:07:33.920
changed on one fateful day.

01:07:33.920 --> 01:07:39.539
It was a seemingly menial task, a job that
could have been done by anyone else, and things

01:07:39.539 --> 01:07:42.140
might have turned out completely different
if they had.

01:07:42.140 --> 01:07:47.700
But yet, in giving it to a certain Junior
Researcher who was in good standing, the Foundation

01:07:47.700 --> 01:07:51.260
completely reoriented the trajectory of Jack’s
life.

01:07:51.260 --> 01:07:57.220
He had been assigned to perform research on
SCP-963, an ornate and rather beautiful amulet

01:07:57.220 --> 01:08:00.059
of unknown origin and purpose.

01:08:00.059 --> 01:08:04.940
The piece was constructed from elegant white
gold, sporting an oval cut ruby surrounded

01:08:04.940 --> 01:08:09.339
by thirteen brilliant cut diamonds in a starburst
pattern.

01:08:09.339 --> 01:08:13.119
It was a truly exquisite and well-crafted
piece of jewelry…

01:08:13.119 --> 01:08:15.539
with a rather strange property to it.

01:08:15.539 --> 01:08:19.970
Not yet realizing the active anomalous effects
of the amulet, Doctor Bright made the honest

01:08:19.970 --> 01:08:24.190
mistake of touching SCP-963 with his bare
hands.

01:08:24.190 --> 01:08:29.449
It was purely an act of human error, avoidable
and yet carrying consequences that would last

01:08:29.449 --> 01:08:32.359
a lifetime… or several.

01:08:32.359 --> 01:08:37.089
You see, as it so happened, the exact same
fateful day that Doctor Bright had made the

01:08:37.089 --> 01:08:43.560
mistake of allowing SCP-963 to make contact
with his skin, another anomaly had simultaneously

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:50.910
managed to breach containment: SCP-076-2,
better known as Able.

01:08:50.910 --> 01:08:55.140
The immortal warrior tore his way through
the Foundation, pulling his bladed weaponry

01:08:55.140 --> 01:08:59.830
out of a pocket dimension and cutting down
any personnel in his path as he continued

01:08:59.830 --> 01:09:00.830
his rampage.

01:09:00.830 --> 01:09:06.199
When the slaughter was over, the dust clearing
and Able re-contained, Junior Researcher Jack

01:09:06.199 --> 01:09:09.820
Bright lay dead, tragically slain during the
breach.

01:09:09.820 --> 01:09:11.710
Or so it first seemed…

01:09:11.710 --> 01:09:16.200
It was during the clean-up in the aftermath
of Able’s rampage, that a member of D Class

01:09:16.200 --> 01:09:21.140
personnel named Tom Higly, was assigned to
dispose of the bodies left in the wake of

01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:23.659
the undying warrior’s massacre.

01:09:23.659 --> 01:09:27.900
That was when Tom unknowingly picked up the
amulet that Doctor Bright had been holding

01:09:27.900 --> 01:09:29.140
when he was killed.

01:09:29.140 --> 01:09:33.920
Instantly, the D Class suffered a peculiar
change, one that would soon become a regular

01:09:33.920 --> 01:09:40.870
occurrence at the SCP Foundation: Tom Higly
became Doctor Jack Bright.

01:09:40.870 --> 01:09:45.560
Once again, like with his brother TJ’s anomalous
powers and the tragedy of his stillborn sister

01:09:45.560 --> 01:09:52.620
SCP-321, the theme of rebirth and regeneration
became a core part of Jack Bright’s life.

01:09:52.620 --> 01:09:56.170
He was alive once again, and inhabiting a
new body.

01:09:56.170 --> 01:10:00.560
Now, this might seem like a positive development
for the aspiring Junior Researcher.

01:10:00.560 --> 01:10:05.550
But, as Jack Bright would soon learn, the
cost of coming back from the dead was a hefty

01:10:05.550 --> 01:10:07.620
toll to pay.

01:10:07.620 --> 01:10:11.540
After the Foundation had established that
the D Class now wearing the amulet was indeed

01:10:11.540 --> 01:10:16.719
the person he claimed to be, they quickly
discovered that removing SCP-963 from the

01:10:16.719 --> 01:10:21.580
wearer would cause all higher brain functions
to shut down immediately.

01:10:21.580 --> 01:10:26.200
Replacing the amulet, the body of Tom Higly
was able to move and speak once again, albeit

01:10:26.200 --> 01:10:29.510
with the voice and consciousness of Jack Bright.

01:10:29.510 --> 01:10:33.480
Through his continued experimentation with
the artifact, Doctor Bright discovered that

01:10:33.480 --> 01:10:39.690
it acted as a vessel for his consciousness,
his collected memories, and his personality.

01:10:39.690 --> 01:10:45.870
They were all seemingly stored within SCP-963
and could be transferred into a new body when

01:10:45.870 --> 01:10:48.210
the amulet was worn by someone else.

01:10:48.210 --> 01:10:52.830
However, part of the high cost of Jack’s
newfound immortality was the immediate loss

01:10:52.830 --> 01:10:56.940
of another person for the sake of his continued
life.

01:10:56.940 --> 01:11:01.500
Placing the amulet on someone else would erase
everything about who they were, effectively

01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:05.120
killing them and wiping them clean to make
room for Jack himself.

01:11:05.120 --> 01:11:09.640
It was only thanks to his mistake of being
the last person to hold the amulet, along

01:11:09.640 --> 01:11:16.190
with being killed by Able directly afterwards,
that had now led to Jack being unable to die.

01:11:16.190 --> 01:11:20.290
Of course, the other large part of the cost
Jack had to pay for living forever was that

01:11:20.290 --> 01:11:26.660
he essentially now had to live as a parasite,
passed on from host to host at a regular rate.

01:11:26.660 --> 01:11:32.690
He learned that, after thirty days of sustained,
continuous contact with SCP-963, Jack’s

01:11:32.690 --> 01:11:35.980
consciousness could become permanently affixed
to a new body.

01:11:35.980 --> 01:11:39.960
If the amulet was removed after this occurred,
then Bright’s consciousness could still

01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:45.889
be passed on to a new body, meaning he could
– in theory, at least – create multiple

01:11:45.889 --> 01:11:51.400
copies of himself by transferring into new
host bodies via the amulet, and allowing them

01:11:51.400 --> 01:11:55.180
to wear it for thirty days at a time to make
the change permanent.

01:11:55.180 --> 01:12:01.210
Naturally, as they so often tend to do, the
SCP Foundation imposed sanctions and restrictions

01:12:01.210 --> 01:12:06.500
to prevent this scenario from unfolding, executing
any hosts wearing the amulet before they could

01:12:06.500 --> 01:12:09.909
reach this thirty-day permanent transfer threshold.

01:12:09.909 --> 01:12:14.449
Afterwards, SCP-963 and Doctor Bright’s
consciousness would be passed on to a new

01:12:14.449 --> 01:12:17.220
host, and the cycle would continue.

01:12:17.220 --> 01:12:22.710
However, after witnessing the immortal researcher’s
continued dedication to their cause, the Foundation

01:12:22.710 --> 01:12:25.830
eventually relaxed its rules for Doctor Bright.

01:12:25.830 --> 01:12:30.370
Jack then went on to continue work with the
Foundation, rising through the ranks and achieving

01:12:30.370 --> 01:12:35.350
promotions to higher stations, eventually
becoming one of the most well-known researchers

01:12:35.350 --> 01:12:37.639
of SCPs to ever live.

01:12:37.639 --> 01:12:42.120
But all the while, he did further examination
of SCP-963.

01:12:42.120 --> 01:12:47.140
You might be fooled into thinking that immortality
would be a gift, but to Jack Bright, it’s

01:12:47.140 --> 01:12:49.690
a never-ending curse.

01:12:49.690 --> 01:12:55.390
Moving from host to host, only surviving because
of a simple mistake that changed his circumstances

01:12:55.390 --> 01:13:00.850
in ways he could never have foreseen, Doctor
Bright lives in frustration and longs to be

01:13:00.850 --> 01:13:04.680
free from what he views as a curse of eternal
life.

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:08.949
He is perpetually aware that he’ll outlive
everyone at the Foundation, have to watch

01:13:08.949 --> 01:13:12.110
countless people die while he lives on.

01:13:12.110 --> 01:13:15.739
Just like he had to live on after the death
of his father.

01:13:15.739 --> 01:13:20.190
This curse of immortality has led Doctor Bright
to long for a permanent death.

01:13:20.190 --> 01:13:27.100
Ironically, he only became a part of SCP-963
in a moment of trying not to die, and now

01:13:27.100 --> 01:13:34.230
can’t at all, making the story of Doctor
Jack Bright a truly saddening tale.

01:13:34.230 --> 01:13:40.150
As history often teaches us, sometimes human
beings are the biggest monsters of all.

01:13:40.150 --> 01:13:45.720
Of course, when you join the ranks of the
SCP Foundation, that starts to seem even less

01:13:45.720 --> 01:13:46.720
likely.

01:13:46.720 --> 01:13:47.830
And why wouldn’t it?

01:13:47.830 --> 01:13:51.750
After all, just look at some of the creatures
they encounter and keep in containment.

01:13:51.750 --> 01:13:57.460
A giant unkillable lizard, a being with toothy
protrusions growing out of its body, a whole

01:13:57.460 --> 01:14:02.760
host of immortal eldritch beings that could
destroy reality, and one sadistic old man

01:14:02.760 --> 01:14:07.610
who drags unsuspecting victims into his own
personal hellscape.

01:14:07.610 --> 01:14:13.090
Monsters are a dime a dozen to the Foundation,
humanity could never be anywhere near as evil

01:14:13.090 --> 01:14:17.310
or as cruel as the real monsters, even on
their worst day.

01:14:17.310 --> 01:14:20.090
Or at least, you’d think.

01:14:20.090 --> 01:14:24.560
Researcher Keltzen had held much of the same
mentality, that the SCP Foundation had the

01:14:24.560 --> 01:14:28.530
job of defending humanity from the real monsters
of the world.

01:14:28.530 --> 01:14:33.030
And it was because of that belief that he
had never previously questioned the Containment

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:36.510
Procedures in place for SCP-1000.

01:14:36.510 --> 01:14:41.800
These beings were a species of nocturnal and
omnivorous apes, usually with bodies covered

01:14:41.800 --> 01:14:46.429
in either grey, brown, black, red or white
fur.

01:14:46.429 --> 01:14:51.260
Possessing the same level of intelligence
as the common chimp, the SCP-1000s had been

01:14:51.260 --> 01:14:56.080
colloquially described as a species known
as ‘Bigfoot’, linking them to the urban

01:14:56.080 --> 01:14:59.550
legend about an ape-like being of dubious
existence.

01:14:59.550 --> 01:15:04.239
Keltzen had hardly been surprised that Bigfoot
itself was an anomalous SCP.

01:15:04.239 --> 01:15:08.720
There were already a number of others that
the Foundation had encountered that seemed

01:15:08.720 --> 01:15:12.050
to have influenced popular folklore from different
parts of the world.

01:15:12.050 --> 01:15:17.030
In fact, it was that unique sub-category of
anomaly that Keltzen was conducting research

01:15:17.030 --> 01:15:21.090
into when he came across the SCP-1000 files.

01:15:21.090 --> 01:15:27.230
He was trying to determine, in various cases,
which came first: the anomaly, or the legend?

01:15:27.230 --> 01:15:33.179
Did the existence of SCP-1000s inspire earlier
human civilisations to pass on stories of

01:15:33.179 --> 01:15:34.340
the Sasquatch?

01:15:34.340 --> 01:15:42.040
Or had those word of mouth tales been somehow
to the very origins of SCP-1000 themselves?

01:15:42.040 --> 01:15:47.370
Studying the files, Keltzen knew there was
a memetic disease infecting hominids like

01:15:47.370 --> 01:15:48.370
SCP-1000.

01:15:48.370 --> 01:15:52.290
The infection meant that anyone who observed
one of the Bigfoot creatures had at minimum

01:15:52.290 --> 01:15:55.780
a 2% chance of suffering instantaneous brain
death.

01:15:55.780 --> 01:16:01.530
It would make discovering the link between
the Sasquatch legends and SCP-1000 difficult,

01:16:01.530 --> 01:16:03.610
not being able to directly look at the apes.

01:16:03.610 --> 01:16:09.139
But Keltzen hoped he wouldn’t need to actually
observe the creatures, only find evidence

01:16:09.139 --> 01:16:15.330
that proved which predated which: SCP-1000
or the legend of Bigfoot.

01:16:15.330 --> 01:16:20.230
The population of SCP-1000 was limited, although
their highest concentrations were known to

01:16:20.230 --> 01:16:24.639
be found in the Pacific Northwest region of
North America, as well as in the Himalayan

01:16:24.639 --> 01:16:26.700
Mountain range in Asia.

01:16:26.700 --> 01:16:31.390
Being stationed at a Foundation facility in
the US, Researcher Keltzen opted to mount

01:16:31.390 --> 01:16:34.090
an expedition to the former location.

01:16:34.090 --> 01:16:39.100
A few odd occurrences had taken place leading
up to his departure: his car tires had been

01:16:39.100 --> 01:16:41.270
slashed, and now needed repairing.

01:16:41.270 --> 01:16:45.500
Newspaper clippings were also left on Keltzen’s
desk that told of various hikers’ deaths

01:16:45.500 --> 01:16:47.620
taking place in the Pacific Northwest.

01:16:47.620 --> 01:16:52.980
A more paranoid person might interpret these
as subtle, indirect threats; it certainly

01:16:52.980 --> 01:16:57.880
seemed as if someone in the SCP Foundation
wasn’t keen on Keltzen departing for his

01:16:57.880 --> 01:16:59.080
research trip.

01:16:59.080 --> 01:17:04.620
Nevertheless, he changed tact and called in
sick to work, sneaking away to a small airstrip

01:17:04.620 --> 01:17:10.070
and boarding a light cargo plane that smuggled
him towards the forests of North America.

01:17:10.070 --> 01:17:12.520
Once he had arrived, night was already falling.

01:17:12.520 --> 01:17:18.010
Keltzen was on his own, traversing the woods
as the sun rapidly receded out of view behind

01:17:18.010 --> 01:17:23.120
the treeline, leaving the forest in darkness,
save for the beam from his flashlight.

01:17:23.120 --> 01:17:28.570
The researcher was hardly an outdoorsy type
and far from an expert at exploring the wilderness,

01:17:28.570 --> 01:17:34.390
but he pushed on into the heart of the woods,
to the areas where the most sightings of SCP-1000

01:17:34.390 --> 01:17:35.690
had been reported.

01:17:35.690 --> 01:17:40.199
It wasn’t long before the torchlight fell
over something imprinted in the woodland floor:

01:17:40.199 --> 01:17:45.320
a large footprint, left by something bipedal
and rather similar to an ape.

01:17:45.320 --> 01:17:49.550
Venturing further, the sound of twigs breaking
caused Keltzen to whirl around.

01:17:49.550 --> 01:17:54.820
The noise had come from nearby, making it
louder than the low, background ambiance of

01:17:54.820 --> 01:17:56.280
the forest at night.

01:17:56.280 --> 01:18:00.090
He swung his flashlight in the direction he
thought it had come from, only seeing the

01:18:00.090 --> 01:18:03.219
shadows of branches cast against the tree
trunks.

01:18:03.219 --> 01:18:09.350
Then, another noise, leaves rustling as something
passed by, followed by more snapping of twigs,

01:18:09.350 --> 01:18:12.020
then the shaking of the leaves again.

01:18:12.020 --> 01:18:15.750
This thing, whatever it might’ve been, was
encircling him.

01:18:15.750 --> 01:18:18.429
Frantically, Keltzen tried to spot it with
his light.

01:18:18.429 --> 01:18:23.930
When, suddenly, the beam fell over something
tall, a hulking figure moving between the

01:18:23.930 --> 01:18:24.940
trees.

01:18:24.940 --> 01:18:29.840
Even from that split second sighting, it was
clearly an instance of SCP-1000.

01:18:29.840 --> 01:18:35.320
The momentary glimpse had reminded the intrepid
researcher of an important detail: he couldn’t

01:18:35.320 --> 01:18:36.320
look at them.

01:18:36.320 --> 01:18:40.961
A 2% chance of instant brain death seemed
too low to worry about, but Keltzen’s research

01:18:40.961 --> 01:18:46.889
had described the effects of the memetic disease
to be cumulative: the longer an SCP-1000 ape

01:18:46.889 --> 01:18:52.150
was observed, the higher the chance of being
killed on the spot by the infection.

01:18:52.150 --> 01:18:57.000
Snapping his eyes shut as tight as he could,
Keltzen was frozen to the spot, at the mercy

01:18:57.000 --> 01:18:59.639
of the sounds surrounding him.

01:18:59.639 --> 01:19:04.949
The SCP-1000 was drawing closer, enough for
the researcher to detect its footsteps.

01:19:04.949 --> 01:19:08.380
His pulse quickened, along with his breathing
to match.

01:19:08.380 --> 01:19:12.590
Although he was already aware that the apes
weren’t known for being aggressive, but

01:19:12.590 --> 01:19:15.900
hearing its heavy breathing as it drew nearer
was enough to make him uncertain.

01:19:15.900 --> 01:19:21.730
Keltzen wasn’t sure if the Bigfoot would
attack him, or pass him by, but by far the

01:19:21.730 --> 01:19:23.850
last thing he had expected…

01:19:23.850 --> 01:19:26.780
was to hear it start speaking.

01:19:26.780 --> 01:19:29.970
“Hu… man,” came a deep, gravely voice.

01:19:29.970 --> 01:19:34.610
It was the kind of sound an animal makes that
can almost be mistaken for speech.

01:19:34.610 --> 01:19:39.360
Keltzen wasn’t fully convinced it had been
a word, rather just his ears playing tricks

01:19:39.360 --> 01:19:41.810
on him in a moment of heightened fear.

01:19:41.810 --> 01:19:44.110
“Human,” it repeated, much clearer.

01:19:44.110 --> 01:19:50.080
There was no mistaking it a second time: the
SCP-1000 had spoken.

01:19:50.080 --> 01:19:51.960
“Y-you can talk?”

01:19:51.960 --> 01:19:54.130
Keltzen exclaimed in disbelief.

01:19:54.130 --> 01:19:59.230
The Sasquatch didn’t answer directly, instead
the researcher detected the sound of receding

01:19:59.230 --> 01:20:00.230
footsteps.

01:20:00.230 --> 01:20:04.480
There was a pause, as the SCP-1000 ape turned
back to Keltzen.

01:20:04.480 --> 01:20:06.660
“Follow, human,” it said.

01:20:06.660 --> 01:20:09.710
“Open your eyes and follow me.”

01:20:09.710 --> 01:20:11.770
“What about the disease?”

01:20:11.770 --> 01:20:14.610
Keltzen shouted, keeping his eyelids still
screwed shut.

01:20:14.610 --> 01:20:19.190
“You’ll be safe,” the ape replied, before
it resumed walking away.

01:20:19.190 --> 01:20:23.330
Cautiously opening his eyes and lifting his
flashlight, Keltzen could see the hairy figure

01:20:23.330 --> 01:20:27.090
making its way through the forest, its back
to him.

01:20:27.090 --> 01:20:32.700
He could see it; the researcher could look
directly at it and yet nothing seemed to be

01:20:32.700 --> 01:20:33.700
happening.

01:20:33.700 --> 01:20:35.910
No adverse side effects, no instant brain
death.

01:20:35.910 --> 01:20:41.760
The odds were low, Keltzen reminded himself,
as he began to warily follow the path of the

01:20:41.760 --> 01:20:42.760
ape.

01:20:42.760 --> 01:20:47.480
Following at a distance, he made sure to momentarily
glance away, trying not to stare for too long

01:20:47.480 --> 01:20:54.060
at the SCP-1000 creature, in the hope that
might disrupt the memetic effects of the infection.

01:20:54.060 --> 01:20:58.081
Before long they had arrived at a cave, deep
in the heart of the forest.

01:20:58.081 --> 01:21:01.800
The embers of a small fire were still aglow
near the rocky entrance.

01:21:01.800 --> 01:21:07.841
As they lumbered inside, the SCP-1000 threw
extra wood on the fire, poking it with a long

01:21:07.841 --> 01:21:10.989
stick until the flames began to reach higher.

01:21:10.989 --> 01:21:14.920
The flickering orange gave Keltzen a better
look at the ape, its large frame, covered

01:21:14.920 --> 01:21:17.139
almost head to toe in brown fur.

01:21:17.139 --> 01:21:23.409
There was something about the creature that
seemed off, not unsettling but almost sad,

01:21:23.409 --> 01:21:24.659
melancholic…

01:21:24.659 --> 01:21:25.820
like it was lonely.

01:21:25.820 --> 01:21:29.969
The researcher had been so amazed that he
could observe the creature for so long without

01:21:29.969 --> 01:21:36.330
dying that he’d barely focused in on the
more important discovery: SCP-1000s were far

01:21:36.330 --> 01:21:42.090
more intelligent than the Foundation files
had indicated, capable of not only speech,

01:21:42.090 --> 01:21:43.350
but emotion too.

01:21:43.350 --> 01:21:45.659
“D-do you have a name?”

01:21:45.659 --> 01:21:47.949
Keltzen stammered, unsure where to start.

01:21:47.949 --> 01:21:49.420
“Grykor,” it replied.

01:21:49.420 --> 01:21:51.390
“That is my name.”

01:21:51.390 --> 01:21:56.130
“You’ll have to forgive me, I’m a little
taken aback by all this,” the researcher

01:21:56.130 --> 01:21:57.480
hastily explained.

01:21:57.480 --> 01:22:01.430
“You’re able to articulate, your species
is advanced enough to have names.”

01:22:01.430 --> 01:22:03.449
“Why should that be surprising?”

01:22:03.449 --> 01:22:04.670
Grykor asked.

01:22:04.670 --> 01:22:08.929
“Well, it’s just, the Foundation seems
to think you and your kind are…

01:22:08.929 --> 01:22:12.530
I don’t mean any offense but, mindless beasts.”

01:22:12.530 --> 01:22:18.040
The ape made a huffing, exhaling sound; Keltzen
noticed after a moment that they were chuckling,

01:22:18.040 --> 01:22:20.950
a dry, almost sarcastic chuckle.

01:22:20.950 --> 01:22:25.320
It was unlike anything he had expected to
learn about SCP-1000.

01:22:25.320 --> 01:22:29.350
That reminded him of why he’d come here.

01:22:29.350 --> 01:22:33.409
“Grykor, may I ask something?” he cleared
his throat.

01:22:33.409 --> 01:22:38.270
Nodding, the SCP-1000 instance poked at the
fire again, paying little mind to the human

01:22:38.270 --> 01:22:39.380
in its company.

01:22:39.380 --> 01:22:41.840
“Where did you and your people come from?”

01:22:41.840 --> 01:22:43.080
Keltzen asked.

01:22:43.080 --> 01:22:44.630
“What’s your history?”

01:22:44.630 --> 01:22:45.630
“Why?”

01:22:45.630 --> 01:22:46.880
Grykor replied abruptly.

01:22:46.880 --> 01:22:54.360
“You see, humans have these legends… stories
about something called Bigfoot, or a Sasquatch.

01:22:54.360 --> 01:22:57.830
The Foundation, the humans I work with, they
call you one and the same.

01:22:57.830 --> 01:23:00.389
I wanted to know if that was true.”

01:23:00.389 --> 01:23:02.480
For a short moment, the creature paused.

01:23:02.480 --> 01:23:06.630
Then, Grykor began to give Keltsen the truth;
the real truth.

01:23:06.630 --> 01:23:12.050
And as they spoke, it was as if the researcher
could see the story unfolding, emphasized

01:23:12.050 --> 01:23:14.679
by the pain in the ape’s teary eyes.

01:23:14.679 --> 01:23:19.290
“We were like you, long, long ago,” it
began explaining.

01:23:19.290 --> 01:23:23.900
“We and humans, we evolved together, side
by side.

01:23:23.900 --> 01:23:26.920
For a time, there was peace between us.

01:23:26.920 --> 01:23:32.540
But we began to grow faster, stronger, became
smarter while you were left behind.”

01:23:32.540 --> 01:23:35.620
“You evolved before early humans did?”

01:23:35.620 --> 01:23:36.989
Keltzen probed.

01:23:36.989 --> 01:23:39.389
“Indeed,” Grykor answered with a gentle
nod.

01:23:39.389 --> 01:23:42.080
“We began to surpass the humans.

01:23:42.080 --> 01:23:47.700
You were still young, still hunting and foraging
for food while we were moving far beyond.

01:23:47.700 --> 01:23:53.650
Thousands and thousands of years, we were
able to develop our culture, our technology,

01:23:53.650 --> 01:23:57.980
all before the humans had even come close
to fire and the wheel.”

01:23:57.980 --> 01:24:00.100
“But that doesn’t make any sense!”

01:24:00.100 --> 01:24:01.980
the researcher interjected yet again.

01:24:01.980 --> 01:24:06.920
“If that was the case, mankind would never
have had its chance to reach the top of the

01:24:06.920 --> 01:24:07.920
food chain.

01:24:07.920 --> 01:24:10.620
We would have died out, and that would have
made you the dominant species on the planet.”

01:24:10.620 --> 01:24:12.820
“We were,” the SCP-1000 ape replied.

01:24:12.820 --> 01:24:14.190
“For a time.”

01:24:14.190 --> 01:24:15.190
“What happened?”

01:24:15.190 --> 01:24:19.560
Grykor turned with a solemn look on their
face.

01:24:19.560 --> 01:24:21.420
“You,” it replied plainly.

01:24:21.420 --> 01:24:22.690
“Humans happened.

01:24:22.690 --> 01:24:27.080
We covered the world, billions and billions
of us.

01:24:27.080 --> 01:24:29.710
You were rare, as few as we are now.

01:24:29.710 --> 01:24:36.010
My people made technology far beyond anything
humans have dreamt up; either before or since.

01:24:36.010 --> 01:24:40.869
Trains, ships, vehicles created from the trees
and the animals.

01:24:40.869 --> 01:24:44.920
Communication using parts of insects and smaller
creatures.

01:24:44.920 --> 01:24:49.969
Cities filled with us, where we all lived
in harmony, stretched out across parts of

01:24:49.969 --> 01:24:52.390
the world humans dared not to tread.”

01:24:52.390 --> 01:24:55.130
“Organic technology,” Keltzen gasped.

01:24:55.130 --> 01:24:56.130
“Fascinating!”

01:24:56.130 --> 01:24:57.770
“We had weapons too.

01:24:57.770 --> 01:25:00.340
And that was our biggest mistake.”

01:25:00.340 --> 01:25:02.170
“What kind of weapons?”

01:25:02.170 --> 01:25:03.820
the researcher quizzed the ape.

01:25:03.820 --> 01:25:07.830
“Given how advanced you were compared to
humanity at that time, I’m assuming your

01:25:07.830 --> 01:25:10.240
weaponry was far beyond our early spears and
clubs.”

01:25:10.240 --> 01:25:16.450
“The devastating kind,” Grykor said, staring
ahead into the fire, almost as if the dancing

01:25:16.450 --> 01:25:21.700
flames conjured up images of the kind of destruction
his ancestor’s weapons could cause.

01:25:21.700 --> 01:25:24.920
“I think I understand,” Keltzen said.

01:25:24.920 --> 01:25:27.719
“There was some kind of conflict, yes?

01:25:27.719 --> 01:25:31.969
A majority of your people perished… oh,
my condolences.

01:25:31.969 --> 01:25:36.110
But that would have removed you from the top
of the evolutionary chain, and allowed the

01:25:36.110 --> 01:25:39.900
earliest ancestors of humanity to usurp your
place there?

01:25:39.900 --> 01:25:45.020
Was the disease you and your species carry
a result of that war, a sort of lingering

01:25:45.020 --> 01:25:47.440
radioactive fallout but on a genetic level?”

01:25:47.440 --> 01:25:50.909
“We are not sick,” Grykor growled.

01:25:50.909 --> 01:25:53.080
“Your people lied.

01:25:53.080 --> 01:25:55.730
They made that up.

01:25:55.730 --> 01:25:57.429
And there was no war…

01:25:57.429 --> 01:25:59.510
it was a slaughter.”

01:25:59.510 --> 01:26:02.750
Keltzen fell quiet, unsure of what to say.

01:26:02.750 --> 01:26:05.480
“Humans would stay away from us back then.

01:26:05.480 --> 01:26:07.060
We avoided them too.

01:26:07.060 --> 01:26:09.550
You slept during the night when we were awake.

01:26:09.550 --> 01:26:10.880
But you made a deal.

01:26:10.880 --> 01:26:14.970
A deal with something ancient and dangerous
in the forests.

01:26:14.970 --> 01:26:17.360
A deal to even the score.

01:26:17.360 --> 01:26:20.970
You found our weapons… and didn’t hesitate.”

01:26:20.970 --> 01:26:26.830
“No, that’s…” the Foundation researcher
tried to protest, but the look of sadness

01:26:26.830 --> 01:26:30.020
on the ape’s face stopped his sentence before
he could finish.

01:26:30.020 --> 01:26:34.940
“We were almost entirely wiped out, in a
single day.

01:26:34.940 --> 01:26:40.230
Humans switched places with us, we became
less and they became more.

01:26:40.230 --> 01:26:45.570
They used our technology to forget about us,
wiped their memories so they wouldn’t have

01:26:45.570 --> 01:26:47.310
to face up to what they had done.

01:26:47.310 --> 01:26:50.040
But we never forgot.”

01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:55.080
“And the Foundation, they maintain that
façade,” Keltzen realised.

01:26:55.080 --> 01:26:59.840
“That’s why they tell us you’re infected,
that if we look at you’ll we’ll die.

01:26:59.840 --> 01:27:01.240
They’re ashamed, we…

01:27:01.240 --> 01:27:02.520
we all should be.”

01:27:02.520 --> 01:27:05.800
“Not just ashamed,” Grykor replied.

01:27:05.800 --> 01:27:06.800
“Afraid.

01:27:06.800 --> 01:27:13.540
They fear we want revenge, retribution for
what was done to our people.”

01:27:13.540 --> 01:27:15.179
“And do you?”

01:27:15.179 --> 01:27:21.239
The ape took another pause, as if it was pondering…
or just allowing itself a moment to feel sad.

01:27:21.239 --> 01:27:24.260
“We have forgiven you,” it answered.

01:27:24.260 --> 01:27:29.719
“I’m not sure we deserve that,” Kletzen
said back, as the pair sat by the fire until

01:27:29.719 --> 01:27:31.490
the sun came up.

01:27:31.490 --> 01:27:36.690
It's rare for the SCP Foundation to encounter
an anomaly that volunteers information about

01:27:36.690 --> 01:27:43.310
itself, that seems to actively desire to be
studied, to be investigated, to be known.

01:27:43.310 --> 01:27:47.330
But SCP-082 is one such anomaly.

01:27:47.330 --> 01:27:53.719
SCP-082 calls himself Fernand, a giant man
with an even bigger appetite, and though he

01:27:53.719 --> 01:27:59.590
is cheerful and chatty, even friendly by SCP
Foundation standards, it can be difficult

01:27:59.590 --> 01:28:03.489
to sort the fact from the fiction where his
life story is concerned.

01:28:03.489 --> 01:28:09.910
At various points in his containment, he has
claimed to be a vampire, a homunculus, Sherlock

01:28:09.910 --> 01:28:15.960
Holmes, Alexander the Great, Dr. Frankenstein
and his Monster, Napoleon, and Big Bird, to

01:28:15.960 --> 01:28:17.160
name a few.

01:28:17.160 --> 01:28:23.650
But on October 27, 2016, Fernand made a particular,
unusual request.

01:28:23.650 --> 01:28:28.320
He asked for a set of poetry books, a stack
of blank paper, and a quill.

01:28:28.320 --> 01:28:34.219
He isolated himself for days, refusing to
eat, drink, or sleep as he wrote page after

01:28:34.219 --> 01:28:35.350
page.

01:28:35.350 --> 01:28:39.790
When he was finished, he had written a poem
that seemed to be the first honest exploration

01:28:39.790 --> 01:28:43.580
of his life story since he was first contained
by the Foundation.

01:28:43.580 --> 01:28:48.969
It can be difficult to determine when SCP-082
is lying, since he is always grinning widely

01:28:48.969 --> 01:28:54.119
and claims to only lie when it is "through
his teeth," but after reading the aforementioned

01:28:54.119 --> 01:29:00.239
poem aloud, he wept openly in what seemed
to be a genuine display of emotion.

01:29:00.239 --> 01:29:04.070
According to the events described in the poem,
which should still be taken with a small grain

01:29:04.070 --> 01:29:10.240
of salt, this is what Fernand's life was like
before he became SCP-082.

01:29:10.240 --> 01:29:16.300
Fernand could not remember his earliest years,
what his parents or his childhood home might

01:29:16.300 --> 01:29:17.340
have looked like.

01:29:17.340 --> 01:29:22.670
His earliest memory was of a makeshift home
in an alleyway, a box to sleep in, and a bundle

01:29:22.670 --> 01:29:25.260
of rags serving as a makeshift bed.

01:29:25.260 --> 01:29:30.060
For food, he would steal day-old bread thrown
out by a nearby bakery, and catch birds and

01:29:30.060 --> 01:29:33.010
rats that were slow enough for him to grab.

01:29:33.010 --> 01:29:38.580
Even as a young boy, he was enormous, three
times the size of other boys his age.

01:29:38.580 --> 01:29:43.010
His strength and stature made him appealing
to the workhouses at first, but they always

01:29:43.010 --> 01:29:47.440
ended up throwing him out, calling him a monster,
le monstre.

01:29:47.440 --> 01:29:51.410
After the third workhouse chased him into
the streets with whips, he resigned himself

01:29:51.410 --> 01:29:56.560
to a solitary life in the shadows, down the
abandoned alleyways where not even the beggars

01:29:56.560 --> 01:29:59.850
would go, so overrun were they were rats and
filth.

01:29:59.850 --> 01:30:05.610
It was better for Fernand that way, no one
would try to mock him, to hurt him, to dispose

01:30:05.610 --> 01:30:07.870
of the monster once and for all.

01:30:07.870 --> 01:30:12.890
He spent his days and nights there, in the
little alleyway he had turned into a home,

01:30:12.890 --> 01:30:16.800
keeping as quiet as possible out of fear that
he would be discovered by someone with ill

01:30:16.800 --> 01:30:18.300
intentions.

01:30:18.300 --> 01:30:22.850
One night, he got his hands on a row of meat
tarts left on a windowsill and ate to his

01:30:22.850 --> 01:30:24.230
heart's content.

01:30:24.230 --> 01:30:28.831
He slept unusually soundly that night, with
a full belly for the first time in a long

01:30:28.831 --> 01:30:33.429
time, and without knowing it, he must have
begun to snore.

01:30:33.429 --> 01:30:38.350
As the first soft beams of sunlight fell across
his face, stirring him from sleep, he heard

01:30:38.350 --> 01:30:40.300
the sound of a strange man's voice.

01:30:40.300 --> 01:30:43.929
Perhaps it was someone just passing by on
a nearby street?

01:30:43.929 --> 01:30:48.690
But no, there was a shadow at the end of the
alleyway, blocking out some of the light.

01:30:48.690 --> 01:30:53.540
He retreated out of sight, hoping to avoid
detection, but he could see two more blurry

01:30:53.540 --> 01:30:55.920
figures stepping into his vision.

01:30:55.920 --> 01:30:59.239
All of a sudden, they started running toward
him at top speed.

01:30:59.239 --> 01:31:04.679
This was no accidental encounter, they had
spotted him and were targeting him specifically.

01:31:04.679 --> 01:31:05.990
For what?

01:31:05.990 --> 01:31:07.880
He didn't want to find out.

01:31:07.880 --> 01:31:12.390
The giant boy climbed to his feet and tried
to run, but the alley was narrow, and the

01:31:12.390 --> 01:31:16.650
three men grabbed hold of each of his arms,
while one grabbed his legs and forced him

01:31:16.650 --> 01:31:17.960
to the ground.

01:31:17.960 --> 01:31:22.230
Someone stuffed a dirty rag into his mouth,
muffling his cries for help, and out of the

01:31:22.230 --> 01:31:26.619
corner of his eye, he saw the glint of a metal
pipe swinging at his head.

01:31:26.619 --> 01:31:28.199
Then, everything went dark.

01:31:28.199 --> 01:31:33.570
When Fernand opened his eyes again, all he
could see at first was red, vivid red on all

01:31:33.570 --> 01:31:34.570
sides.

01:31:34.570 --> 01:31:37.800
As his vision adjusted, he realized he could
see metal bars as well.

01:31:37.800 --> 01:31:43.290
The red was fabric, bright cloth draped over
the iron cage that now held him prisoner.

01:31:43.290 --> 01:31:48.620
He stumbled to his feet, forced to stoop to
avoid bumping his head on the top of the cage,

01:31:48.620 --> 01:31:50.230
and rattled the bars desperately.

01:31:50.230 --> 01:31:51.659
"Let me out!

01:31:51.659 --> 01:31:52.659
Please!"

01:31:52.659 --> 01:31:55.219
He begged, though he felt as though it was
useless.

01:31:55.219 --> 01:31:59.460
His plea was not met with silence, however,
but with loud, cheerful music.

01:31:59.460 --> 01:32:05.280
He covered his ears at the sudden noise, jumping
back, but it came from all sides, and there

01:32:05.280 --> 01:32:06.430
was no escape.

01:32:06.430 --> 01:32:10.960
Suddenly, his eyes were blinded by a sudden
bright light as the red cloth was yanked from

01:32:10.960 --> 01:32:12.310
the cage in one swoop.

01:32:12.310 --> 01:32:17.350
It took a moment for his eyes to adjust, but
as he blinked the tears from his eyes, Fernand

01:32:17.350 --> 01:32:23.440
could make out a man with a large mustache,
a long red and gold coat, a matching top hat,

01:32:23.440 --> 01:32:25.730
and a gold-tipped cane.

01:32:25.730 --> 01:32:29.410
The man grinned at him, but it was not a friendly
face.

01:32:29.410 --> 01:32:34.250
The smile did not reach his eyes, which glinted
with a cold, cruel delight.

01:32:34.250 --> 01:32:39.691
Behind the ringmaster, Fernand could now see
a crowd sitting: men, women, and children

01:32:39.691 --> 01:32:46.120
all watching him with rapturous fascination,
a mix of intrigue, disgust, and horror on

01:32:46.120 --> 01:32:47.120
their faces.

01:32:47.120 --> 01:32:53.570
He looked from face to face, seeking any kindness,
any sympathy, but found not one who looked

01:32:53.570 --> 01:32:55.889
as if they might help him escape.

01:32:55.889 --> 01:33:00.590
The music climbed to a crescendo, and when
it stopped, the ringmaster spoke.

01:33:00.590 --> 01:33:05.869
"Ladies and gentlemen, boys and girls, today
I have brought you something the likes of

01:33:05.869 --> 01:33:08.199
which no one has ever seen before!

01:33:08.199 --> 01:33:14.180
I have traveled the world in search of oddities
and curiosities to delight and shock you with,

01:33:14.180 --> 01:33:19.929
and after crossing every ocean, exploring
the furthest reaches of the globe, I have

01:33:19.929 --> 01:33:22.340
brought you a very special gift!

01:33:22.340 --> 01:33:25.139
Do not be afraid, the beast will not harm
you!

01:33:25.139 --> 01:33:28.380
I will ensure that you are all perfectly safe.

01:33:28.380 --> 01:33:33.510
Should he try anything at all, one of my helpers
here," he gestured to one of two large men

01:33:33.510 --> 01:33:39.070
standing on either side of the cage, each
brandishing an ax, "Will simply strike him

01:33:39.070 --> 01:33:40.280
with their axes!"

01:33:40.280 --> 01:33:45.489
Fernand recoiled at the threat, but there
was nowhere for him to go in the cramped cage,

01:33:45.489 --> 01:33:48.110
already a bit too small for his massive body.

01:33:48.110 --> 01:33:53.060
The ringmaster continued his speech, pacing
back and forth in front of the cage and running

01:33:53.060 --> 01:33:56.550
his cane across the bars with a horrible clattering
sound.

01:33:56.550 --> 01:34:02.449
"As you can see, this creature looks like
a boy, but his statue reveals the truth: it

01:34:02.449 --> 01:34:05.260
is something else entirely!

01:34:05.260 --> 01:34:09.580
Something bigger, stronger, more vicious than
any child could ever be!

01:34:09.580 --> 01:34:14.619
You see the legs like great tree trunks, the
arms strong enough to wrestle a bear!

01:34:14.619 --> 01:34:19.310
But do not, dear audience, neglect to notice
its mighty jaws!

01:34:19.310 --> 01:34:24.780
Like steel, strong enough to crush anything
it bites down on, strong enough to crack through

01:34:24.780 --> 01:34:25.780
bone!

01:34:25.780 --> 01:34:31.080
And the teeth...enormous and pearly white,
with a smile bright enough to light up the

01:34:31.080 --> 01:34:32.180
darkness.

01:34:32.180 --> 01:34:35.890
What do you say, beast, will you give the
people a smile?"

01:34:35.890 --> 01:34:40.040
Fernand cowered in the cage, watching everything
with wide, frightened eyes.

01:34:40.040 --> 01:34:42.830
He certainly did not feel like smiling.

01:34:42.830 --> 01:34:47.219
When he did not budge, the ringmaster thwacked
his cane against the bars, making another

01:34:47.219 --> 01:34:49.380
horrible metallic clang.

01:34:49.380 --> 01:34:50.380
"Well?

01:34:50.380 --> 01:34:54.410
These people paid good money to be here, do
not keep them waiting!"

01:34:54.410 --> 01:34:58.630
After another moment of silence and Fernand
remaining stock still, the audience began

01:34:58.630 --> 01:35:02.969
to boo, tossing empty popcorn bags and insults
at the cage.

01:35:02.969 --> 01:35:08.320
The ringmaster leaned in close and whispered,
"Smile for the people, or I'll see to it you

01:35:08.320 --> 01:35:11.119
suffer agony you could only dream of."

01:35:11.119 --> 01:35:15.511
This threat snapped Fernand out of his frozen
terror, and, standing as tall as he could

01:35:15.511 --> 01:35:21.440
in the little cage, he smiled wide, showing
all of his many, many teeth at once.

01:35:21.440 --> 01:35:26.180
The audience erupted into cheers at the sight,
and the band played triumphant horns as the

01:35:26.180 --> 01:35:28.400
ringmaster basked in the applause.

01:35:28.400 --> 01:35:30.510
"How about that?"

01:35:30.510 --> 01:35:34.510
The ringmaster took off his hat and bowed
low to the ground, holding out the hat for

01:35:34.510 --> 01:35:39.850
the audience to toss in coins in a token of
their appreciation for an excellent show.

01:35:39.850 --> 01:35:44.310
By the time the applause was finished, his
hat was filled with shiny silver and gold.

01:35:44.310 --> 01:35:49.420
He tossed the hat, now heavy with new wealth,
to one of the guards, and pulled a gold-handled

01:35:49.420 --> 01:35:50.920
whip from his pocket.

01:35:50.920 --> 01:35:53.409
"Ah, but I haven't finished yet!"

01:35:53.409 --> 01:35:57.030
He cracked the whip ominously, then turned
toward the cage.

01:35:57.030 --> 01:36:00.080
He cracked the whip again, this time across
Fernand's back.

01:36:00.080 --> 01:36:05.070
The boy roared in pain, a great bellowing
sound that carried for miles around, and fell

01:36:05.070 --> 01:36:06.380
to his knees.

01:36:06.380 --> 01:36:11.300
The sound was so loud, so powerful, that the
audience was terrified.

01:36:11.300 --> 01:36:16.180
Small children began to cry, and people poured
from the circus tent in a panic, afraid the

01:36:16.180 --> 01:36:18.730
beast would break free and tear them apart.

01:36:18.730 --> 01:36:23.710
But there was no beast, only a frightened
boy who did not understand his own strength,

01:36:23.710 --> 01:36:28.199
crying at the cruelty the world had shown
him all his life.

01:36:28.199 --> 01:36:32.890
Once they were alone, the ringmaster turned
to Fernand again, catching his eye.

01:36:32.890 --> 01:36:35.130
"Stop your crying, boy.

01:36:35.130 --> 01:36:37.280
From here on out, you will be coming with
me.

01:36:37.280 --> 01:36:40.199
Together, we're going to be very, very rich.

01:36:40.199 --> 01:36:43.679
And I will make you a star.

01:36:43.679 --> 01:36:46.010
Whether you want to be one or not."

01:36:46.010 --> 01:36:48.580
And for a time, that became Fernand's life.

01:36:48.580 --> 01:36:53.699
Night after night, crammed into a cage, fed
on chicken bones and leftover scraps, paraded

01:36:53.699 --> 01:36:59.460
in front of uncaring audiences as the ringmaster
tormented him, showed him off as a sideshow

01:36:59.460 --> 01:37:03.719
attraction, and forced him to smile with the
threat of the ax or the whip.

01:37:03.719 --> 01:37:07.420
His fellow circus folk were not much kinder
than the ringmaster, either.

01:37:07.420 --> 01:37:10.980
The clowns sprayed him with water and taunted
him through the bars.

01:37:10.980 --> 01:37:15.679
The animal trainers threatened to pit him
against their lions and see who came out alive.

01:37:15.679 --> 01:37:21.330
Only one person ever showed Fernand an ounce
of kindness at the circus, a bearded lady

01:37:21.330 --> 01:37:25.250
who would sing opera songs in a beautiful,
clear soprano.

01:37:25.250 --> 01:37:30.270
Her voice was the loveliest thing Fernand
had ever heard, a tiny slice of heaven in

01:37:30.270 --> 01:37:32.760
the hell his life had become.

01:37:32.760 --> 01:37:38.020
Days bled together as the tour went on, a
stretch of seemingly endless days and nights,

01:37:38.020 --> 01:37:40.900
until the day of the last show came.

01:37:40.900 --> 01:37:45.120
He looked up at the thin slivers of night
sky visible through the gaps in the tent,

01:37:45.120 --> 01:37:47.890
the twinkling of stars just out of reach.

01:37:47.890 --> 01:37:50.420
What would become of him after the tour ended?

01:37:50.420 --> 01:37:52.030
What horrors awaited him next?

01:37:52.030 --> 01:37:56.409
Would he just sit there and take it, or would
he finally do something to bring it all to

01:37:56.409 --> 01:37:57.409
a close?

01:37:57.409 --> 01:38:02.949
He flexed his muscles quietly, taking notice
of how he had grown over the past few months.

01:38:02.949 --> 01:38:07.770
Regular feedings, more than he had gotten
on the street, had bulked him up considerably.

01:38:07.770 --> 01:38:13.050
He now stood at over seven feet tall, and
could feel that his strength had doubled,

01:38:13.050 --> 01:38:14.659
or even tripled.

01:38:14.659 --> 01:38:18.940
The show began as it usually did, with the
crack of the ringmaster's whip.

01:38:18.940 --> 01:38:23.280
But suddenly, the man stepped forward and
began a speech he had never made before.

01:38:23.280 --> 01:38:27.070
"As you can see, this great beast cowers at
the sound of my whip!

01:38:27.070 --> 01:38:31.880
Over the months, I have trained it, domesticated
it until it was aggressive no longer!

01:38:31.880 --> 01:38:35.000
Behold, as I set it free!"

01:38:35.000 --> 01:38:40.440
And, to Fernand's shock, the ringmaster unlocked
the door to the cage and swung it open.

01:38:40.440 --> 01:38:45.330
He took a tentative step out, standing at
his full height for the first time in months

01:38:45.330 --> 01:38:47.320
as he stretched his spine straight.

01:38:47.320 --> 01:38:49.260
"See how it is tamed!

01:38:49.260 --> 01:38:50.820
Go on now, boy!

01:38:50.820 --> 01:38:52.370
Smile for your public!

01:38:52.370 --> 01:38:54.550
They've been so good to us."

01:38:54.550 --> 01:38:59.010
He jabbed Fernand's ribs with his cane, hard
enough to leave a dark purple bruise.

01:38:59.010 --> 01:39:00.969
"You heard me, smile!"

01:39:00.969 --> 01:39:05.810
The whip cracked again, and the audience began
to boo and jeer, throwing their garbage at

01:39:05.810 --> 01:39:06.810
Fernand.

01:39:06.810 --> 01:39:11.699
A bag hit his cheek with a slap, and a deep,
dark, rage finally awoke beneath the months

01:39:11.699 --> 01:39:12.699
of terror.

01:39:12.699 --> 01:39:17.490
"You remember our agreement, do as I say,
or I shall cut off something you’ll miss!"

01:39:17.490 --> 01:39:22.110
Before the ringmaster could say another word,
Fernand grabbed him and pulled him into a

01:39:22.110 --> 01:39:24.119
bone-crushing embrace.

01:39:24.119 --> 01:39:29.679
The man's voice came out in a squeak, "Beast,
what are you doing?"

01:39:29.679 --> 01:39:30.679
"That!

01:39:30.679 --> 01:39:31.679
Is!

01:39:31.679 --> 01:39:32.679
Not!

01:39:32.679 --> 01:39:33.679
My name!"

01:39:33.679 --> 01:39:38.580
Fernand bellowed, and he opened his mouth
wide, wider than he ever had before, and gobbled

01:39:38.580 --> 01:39:41.679
up the ringmaster in three quick bites.

01:39:41.679 --> 01:39:46.290
The audience screamed, fleeing from their
seats, but Fernand moved faster than they

01:39:46.290 --> 01:39:47.290
did.

01:39:47.290 --> 01:39:51.619
He grabbed person after person, starting with
those that had been the cruelest and shouted

01:39:51.619 --> 01:39:56.550
the most insulting things, and devoured them
one by one.

01:39:56.550 --> 01:40:02.110
When he was full, he ran into the night, into
the forest, and did not stop until he was

01:40:02.110 --> 01:40:03.110
free.

01:40:03.110 --> 01:40:07.260
He bathed in the river, washing away all that
he had done, and remained there for quite

01:40:07.260 --> 01:40:11.440
some time, feeding on passersby when his hunger
got the better of him.

01:40:11.440 --> 01:40:16.250
His sense of time grew fuzzy then, and he
could not be sure how long he spent there.

01:40:16.250 --> 01:40:21.739
But word of missing townspeople eventually
reached a special organization, one dedicated

01:40:21.739 --> 01:40:27.320
to studying those like him, and for the second
time in his life, Fernand was taken into custody

01:40:27.320 --> 01:40:28.800
by strangers.

01:40:28.800 --> 01:40:34.020
However, these scientists were much kinder
to him, and never called him a beast.

01:40:34.020 --> 01:40:39.449
They simply gave him a number, SCP-082.

01:40:39.449 --> 01:40:44.230
Doctor Trebuchet sighed, his breath fogging
up the visor of his hazmat suit.

01:40:44.230 --> 01:40:48.989
He did a last-minute spot check, making sure
he had his clipboard and pen.

01:40:48.989 --> 01:40:54.270
Then, he reached for the shoe box, feeling
its little occupant moving around as he lifted

01:40:54.270 --> 01:40:55.270
it up.

01:40:55.270 --> 01:41:00.530
The doctor turned as his newly appointed assistant,
Researcher Wachtel, barrelled into the room.

01:41:00.530 --> 01:41:06.889
The researcher was late; Trebuchet couldn’t
imagine ever being tardy to his first assignment.

01:41:06.889 --> 01:41:12.130
But the Foundation were insistent on training
up their new recruits as quickly as possible.

01:41:12.130 --> 01:41:17.540
Exposure to a mostly harmless anomaly would,
in theory, help to normalize some of the stranger

01:41:17.540 --> 01:41:20.060
SCPs that the organization dealt with.

01:41:20.060 --> 01:41:25.380
Doctor Trebuchet held back a cheap shot about
Wachtel being so late, as the Assistant Researcher

01:41:25.380 --> 01:41:28.750
hurriedly clambered into his own hazmat suit.

01:41:28.750 --> 01:41:33.920
Both ready, they stepped through the decontamination
airlock after it had hissed loudly, sterilizing

01:41:33.920 --> 01:41:36.650
the air and the outer layers of their suits.

01:41:36.650 --> 01:41:40.820
Then, the two researchers continued into the
containment unit.

01:41:40.820 --> 01:41:45.900
Even with their hazmat suits on, the differences
in temperature and humidity were instantly

01:41:45.900 --> 01:41:50.300
noticeable, like the feeling you get stepping
off a plane after a long-haul flight.

01:41:50.300 --> 01:41:55.949
The cylindrical glass enclosure had been perfectly
climate controlled, filled with wetland soil

01:41:55.949 --> 01:41:57.409
and stagnant water.

01:41:57.409 --> 01:42:02.790
It was like stepping inside a terrarium, a
tiny little pocket of swamp large enough for

01:42:02.790 --> 01:42:03.790
someone…

01:42:03.790 --> 01:42:07.580
well, something to comfortably live inside.

01:42:07.580 --> 01:42:13.761
That something was SCP-811, sometimes known
colloquially by her nickname of the Swamp

01:42:13.761 --> 01:42:14.761
Woman.

01:42:14.761 --> 01:42:20.400
However, since bringing her into containment,
SCP-811 had given herself a different name,

01:42:20.400 --> 01:42:24.010
which Doctor Trebuchet used when addressing
the slimy creature.

01:42:24.010 --> 01:42:29.530
“Aé,” he greeted, causing SCP-811 to
turn and face the pair of Foundation personnel.

01:42:29.530 --> 01:42:31.030
“Dockty, what want?”

01:42:31.030 --> 01:42:35.690
she hissed – she had shown the capacity
for communication, but possessed a limited

01:42:35.690 --> 01:42:40.340
vocabulary and struggled with the proper pronunciation
of words.

01:42:40.340 --> 01:42:45.390
Doctor Trebuchet clarified he was there to
ask SCP-811 questions, to which she quickly

01:42:45.390 --> 01:42:48.110
responded with a request for food.

01:42:48.110 --> 01:42:52.560
Opening up the shoebox, a frog hopped out,
which the Swamp Woman quickly stomped on and

01:42:52.560 --> 01:42:54.790
began to eat the dead creature.

01:42:54.790 --> 01:42:59.180
Although, while she possessed a mouth, she
didn’t exactly ‘eat’ in the sense that

01:42:59.180 --> 01:43:00.560
we understand it.

01:43:00.560 --> 01:43:06.659
Her body secrets a slimy mucus that, upon
contact, causes a rapid rate of decomposition.

01:43:06.659 --> 01:43:12.290
Much like a fly, SCP-811 will vomit onto things
in order to break them down so she can digest

01:43:12.290 --> 01:43:14.580
their nutrients through absorption.

01:43:14.580 --> 01:43:20.090
She uses this ability both to hunt, and as
a defensive countermeasure against attackers,

01:43:20.090 --> 01:43:23.610
spitting out the waste of her food that collects
in her stomach.

01:43:23.610 --> 01:43:28.969
Grinning at her own efforts, SCP-811 finished
her meal and indicated she was ready to continue

01:43:28.969 --> 01:43:30.360
with questioning.

01:43:30.360 --> 01:43:34.949
Doctor Trebuchet handed his clipboard to Researcher
Wachtel so that he could conduct the interview

01:43:34.949 --> 01:43:37.110
as part of his on-the-job training.

01:43:37.110 --> 01:43:39.830
“What is the earliest thing you can remember?”

01:43:39.830 --> 01:43:42.159
Wachtel asked, reading from the board.

01:43:42.159 --> 01:43:44.370
“Air-wee is thing?”

01:43:44.370 --> 01:43:48.250
SCP-811 echoed; conveying that she didn’t
fully understand.

01:43:48.250 --> 01:43:50.880
Wachtel stammered, unsure how to rephrase.

01:43:50.880 --> 01:43:53.820
“Early things,” Doctor Trebuchet interjected.

01:43:53.820 --> 01:43:54.820
“Things…

01:43:54.820 --> 01:43:55.820
before.”

01:43:55.820 --> 01:43:57.230
“Before box?”

01:43:57.230 --> 01:44:01.619
Aé seemed to be understanding, indicating
her containment chamber.

01:44:01.619 --> 01:44:05.500
“Before before box,” Trebuchet confirmed.

01:44:05.500 --> 01:44:10.310
Appearing thoughtful for a moment, SCP-811
asked: “Most before?”

01:44:10.310 --> 01:44:15.070
Doctor Trebuchet nodded vigorously, telling
her ‘Yes’; even though the Foundation

01:44:15.070 --> 01:44:20.370
had mandated this interview, he genuinely
wanted to know how she had become like this.

01:44:20.370 --> 01:44:25.580
Immediately, SCP-811 gave sweeping, demonstrative
gestures with her hands and described – in

01:44:25.580 --> 01:44:28.619
very vague levels of detail – a man.

01:44:28.619 --> 01:44:34.099
A man who had been taller than Aé herself,
at a time when she was also much, much smaller.

01:44:34.099 --> 01:44:37.580
“Was like man,” she said, pointing at
her own arm.

01:44:37.580 --> 01:44:39.250
“What was like him?”

01:44:39.250 --> 01:44:42.590
Doctor Trebuchet probed, trying to clarify
what she meant.

01:44:42.590 --> 01:44:47.300
In response, Aé pinched her mottled green
skin, its surface slick with mucus.

01:44:47.300 --> 01:44:48.300
“This!

01:44:48.300 --> 01:44:49.739
Not like Aé.

01:44:49.739 --> 01:44:50.920
Like man.

01:44:50.920 --> 01:44:52.020
Like people!”

01:44:52.020 --> 01:44:53.070
she insisted.

01:44:53.070 --> 01:44:57.040
Her skin, she had meant her skin had used
to look human.

01:44:57.040 --> 01:45:02.390
Upon hearing the word‘ skin’ from the
doctor, SCP-811 repeated it, prodding at her

01:45:02.390 --> 01:45:05.870
arm, and then smiled; she’d learned a new
word.

01:45:05.870 --> 01:45:10.239
When questioned further by Doctor Trebuchet
– while Wachtel felt increasingly uneasy

01:45:10.239 --> 01:45:14.160
nearby – the Swamp Woman pointed to the
inside of her elbow.

01:45:14.160 --> 01:45:18.400
She described what she had experienced after
this tall man had approached her:

01:45:18.400 --> 01:45:20.449
“Pain stick here.

01:45:20.449 --> 01:45:22.500
Cold,” Aé said.

01:45:22.500 --> 01:45:27.130
“Needle, Aé,” Doctor Trebuchet encouraged
her to use the right word, certain she meant

01:45:27.130 --> 01:45:28.920
she had been injected with something.

01:45:28.920 --> 01:45:30.590
“They’re called needles.”

01:45:30.590 --> 01:45:36.360
“Knee-doll here,” SCP-811 tried to mimic
the sounds of the word, before then going

01:45:36.360 --> 01:45:40.320
on to explain that she had experienced intense
pain soon after.

01:45:40.320 --> 01:45:43.840
With her hands, she started miming that something
had come out of her mouth.

01:45:43.840 --> 01:45:44.840
“Red.

01:45:44.840 --> 01:45:46.190
Red, red, red.

01:45:46.190 --> 01:45:48.469
Was… very hunger-y.

01:45:48.469 --> 01:45:50.020
Ate man.

01:45:50.020 --> 01:45:52.349
Skin… like this.”

01:45:52.349 --> 01:45:56.940
Hearing her describe, even in loose, broken
terminology, what had happened to her, became

01:45:56.940 --> 01:45:59.400
too much for Assistant Researcher Wachtel.

01:45:59.400 --> 01:46:04.510
As far as both he and Doctor Trebuchet understood
what Aé was telling them, she had been injected

01:46:04.510 --> 01:46:09.060
with something while still a child, and then
had… eaten the person responsible.

01:46:09.060 --> 01:46:14.130
Wachtel, who had children of his own, couldn’t
help but imagine the same horrible act happening

01:46:14.130 --> 01:46:18.790
to his own kids; and the thought of it made
him vomit in his hazmat suit.

01:46:18.790 --> 01:46:23.610
Given her own chosen method of attacking and
dissolving prey, SCP-811 couldn’t help that

01:46:23.610 --> 01:46:27.170
she interpreted seeing Wachtel throwing up
as an act of hostility.

01:46:27.170 --> 01:46:31.420
It alarmed the Swamp Woman, causing her to
hurriedly retreat to a nearby pool of stagnant

01:46:31.420 --> 01:46:36.179
water within her containment chamber, refusing
to remerge until Wachtel and Doctor Trebuchet

01:46:36.179 --> 01:46:37.239
had left.

01:46:37.239 --> 01:46:41.570
Angered by the Assistant Researcher’s lack
of professionalism and how it had interrupted

01:46:41.570 --> 01:46:47.010
the most coherent explanation of SCP-811’s
past, Doctor trebuchet filed a dissatisfied

01:46:47.010 --> 01:46:48.900
note with the SCP Foundation.

01:46:48.900 --> 01:46:52.300
It read:
“While I understand the appeal of training

01:46:52.300 --> 01:46:56.449
the new blood on something relatively harmless
and as green as they are, could you please

01:46:56.449 --> 01:46:58.969
throw them at some other humanoid for a while?

01:46:58.969 --> 01:47:01.090
I'm trying to actually get things done here.”

01:47:01.090 --> 01:47:06.170
Had Wachtel been able to stomach what he had
heard SCP-811 saying, and probably through

01:47:06.170 --> 01:47:10.480
more extensive interviews with the creature,
Trebuchet could have learned the full extent

01:47:10.480 --> 01:47:12.699
of how the Swamp Woman had come to be.

01:47:12.699 --> 01:47:17.300
She had been able to give the broad strokes
of it, of course, but her descriptive ability

01:47:17.300 --> 01:47:18.460
was limited.

01:47:18.460 --> 01:47:22.670
The story had definitely fit with what the
Foundation already assumed to be the case;

01:47:22.670 --> 01:47:25.230
that, at some point, Aé had been human.

01:47:25.230 --> 01:47:30.190
But what they hadn’t been aware of was the
actions of this unknown individual – a nameless

01:47:30.190 --> 01:47:36.140
man – or what he had injected the young
girl with in order to trigger her transformation.

01:47:36.140 --> 01:47:41.030
At any rate, the only explanation the Foundation
had was the word of SCP-811 herself.

01:47:41.030 --> 01:47:46.610
It was vague at best; at worst it might not
have even been recalled with any degree of

01:47:46.610 --> 01:47:47.610
accuracy.

01:47:47.610 --> 01:47:51.680
She could have been lying, if she even had
the capacity to lie.

01:47:51.680 --> 01:47:57.350
And the specifics of who she was before, why
someone had injected her and what with, were

01:47:57.350 --> 01:48:00.460
all still a complete mystery to the Foundation.

01:48:00.460 --> 01:48:02.610
And not just to them either.

01:48:02.610 --> 01:48:07.710
Given her partial comprehension, SCP-811 had
long been suspected to have grown up as a

01:48:07.710 --> 01:48:12.710
feral child, a youngster abandoned when they
were only a few years of age, rather than

01:48:12.710 --> 01:48:14.150
as an infant.

01:48:14.150 --> 01:48:18.300
It certainly seemed to explain her limited
understanding of human language; the fact

01:48:18.300 --> 01:48:23.610
she could communicate at all implied she had,
at some point, lived among other humans, but

01:48:23.610 --> 01:48:26.650
hadn’t fully developed before her transformation.

01:48:26.650 --> 01:48:31.520
But even if she had been able to communicate
at an adult level, SCP-811 still wouldn’t

01:48:31.520 --> 01:48:34.260
have given the full story to Doctor Trebuchet.

01:48:34.260 --> 01:48:39.369
Not because she was keeping things from him,
but because she simply couldn’t.

01:48:39.369 --> 01:48:43.790
The trauma of growing up alone in the swamp,
being turned into a monster, and everything

01:48:43.790 --> 01:48:47.210
else that had come before, were all too much
for her.

01:48:47.210 --> 01:48:51.670
She had, at some point, suffered a mental
break, her mind shutting off the full details

01:48:51.670 --> 01:48:54.099
of her past as a defense mechanism.

01:48:54.099 --> 01:48:59.400
The Swamp Woman had been through far more
– and far worse – than even she could

01:48:59.400 --> 01:49:00.400
remember.

01:49:00.400 --> 01:49:05.159
And, unbeknownst to the girl who would later
come to call herself Aé, the SCP Foundation

01:49:05.159 --> 01:49:06.159
had been involved…

01:49:06.159 --> 01:49:11.909
It had all started way back, a long time ago,
and many, many miles away from where it would

01:49:11.909 --> 01:49:14.730
eventually lead to the birth of the Swamp
Woman.

01:49:14.730 --> 01:49:17.610
Specifically, it began in Siberia.

01:49:17.610 --> 01:49:22.040
The SCP Foundation had learned of an infectious
skin disease that had been discovered there

01:49:22.040 --> 01:49:27.929
by GRU Division P, the Russian government’s
own anomaly containment organization.

01:49:27.929 --> 01:49:32.170
They had encountered a virus that was highly
contagious, and that would spread on direct

01:49:32.170 --> 01:49:34.030
contact with organic matter.

01:49:34.030 --> 01:49:36.820
But that was far from the extent of it.

01:49:36.820 --> 01:49:41.350
This infection would begin to transform its
host, turning them into a horrific fleshy

01:49:41.350 --> 01:49:46.210
mutation that would attack any non-infected
life form in an aggressive attempt to spread

01:49:46.210 --> 01:49:47.469
the virus further.

01:49:47.469 --> 01:49:52.960
The Foundation called it SCP-610, although
the GRU P troops had a different name for

01:49:52.960 --> 01:49:57.010
it: roughly translated, they called it ‘The
Flesh That Hates’.

01:49:57.010 --> 01:50:01.660
Now, it was only through a combined effort
between the SCP Foundation and their Russian

01:50:01.660 --> 01:50:07.940
counterparts in GRU Division P that this disease
was contained to a single, isolated area within

01:50:07.940 --> 01:50:10.500
Siberia where it had first been discovered.

01:50:10.500 --> 01:50:15.000
The Russian government at the time had agreed
to keep the area under their watchful eye,

01:50:15.000 --> 01:50:19.960
and that anyone who became infected by SCP-610
would be killed immediately.

01:50:19.960 --> 01:50:23.260
Of course, nobody had accounted for the one
who would get away.

01:50:23.260 --> 01:50:29.520
A Russian operative, a researcher within GRU
P, had become obsessed with SCP-610 since

01:50:29.520 --> 01:50:30.969
its discovery.

01:50:30.969 --> 01:50:37.239
The virus itself was horrific, producing abominable
nightmares, but it could transform human physiology

01:50:37.239 --> 01:50:42.580
in mere moments, producing fleshy creatures
that were almost unrecognizable as people.

01:50:42.580 --> 01:50:48.780
This scientist began to wonder, if this transformative
property of the virus could somehow be controlled,

01:50:48.780 --> 01:50:53.130
programmed even, then it may well hold the
key to some of the greatest breakthroughs

01:50:53.130 --> 01:50:54.699
in medical science.

01:50:54.699 --> 01:50:59.440
He envisioned using it to grow replacement
limbs out of tissue cells, or to trigger the

01:50:59.440 --> 01:51:02.280
reconstructive regeneration of bodily damage.

01:51:02.280 --> 01:51:07.739
The scientist just needed to figure out how
to prevent SCP-610 from fully transforming

01:51:07.739 --> 01:51:12.210
someone; the only problem was anyone infected
with it was killed.

01:51:12.210 --> 01:51:17.219
So, he took it upon himself to sneak into
the isolated area in Siberia, where he could

01:51:17.219 --> 01:51:21.829
steal a sample of SCP-610 from the body of
a recently terminated mutation.

01:51:21.829 --> 01:51:25.219
And he nearly made it away unscathed…

01:51:25.219 --> 01:51:29.620
The scientist’s plans were quickly discovered
by the joint effort between the SCP Foundation

01:51:29.620 --> 01:51:34.909
and GRU P. He had been requesting a sample
through the official channels for months,

01:51:34.909 --> 01:51:39.909
and now he had taken one to experiment with
and gone on the run with a new identity.

01:51:39.909 --> 01:51:44.350
Calling himself ‘Alec Holland’, the scientist
fled the Foundation-Russian agents sent to

01:51:44.350 --> 01:51:45.430
pursue him.

01:51:45.430 --> 01:51:49.599
As far as he knew, they were trying to prevent
him from carrying out what he viewed to be

01:51:49.599 --> 01:51:50.599
a noble endeavor.

01:51:50.599 --> 01:51:56.159
But the Foundation knew that, if even that
tiny sample of SCP-610 was able to spread,

01:51:56.159 --> 01:51:58.310
it could cause a worldwide catastrophe.

01:51:58.310 --> 01:52:03.989
Whilst piloting a small plane, Alec was shot
down over the swamplands of Louisiana.

01:52:03.989 --> 01:52:08.200
By the time Foundation operatives arrived
in the area, all they found was a burning

01:52:08.200 --> 01:52:09.200
wreck.

01:52:09.200 --> 01:52:13.349
Little did they realize, Alec had been rescued
by locals, who had taken him aboard their

01:52:13.349 --> 01:52:15.849
fan boat and rushed him to hospital.

01:52:15.849 --> 01:52:19.699
He would later leave, before his injuries
had fully healed, and returned to the site

01:52:19.699 --> 01:52:22.869
of the crash to discover something curious.

01:52:22.869 --> 01:52:28.330
His sample of SCP-610 had been cross-contaminated
with the swamp’s stagnant water, teeming

01:52:28.330 --> 01:52:31.290
with algae and who knows what else.

01:52:31.290 --> 01:52:32.690
Alec was running out of time.

01:52:32.690 --> 01:52:36.889
He knew that, if his wounds didn’t kill
him, he would soon be discovered by the Foundation

01:52:36.889 --> 01:52:42.219
and GRU P. Using a syringe, he gathered up
the combination of the virus sample that had

01:52:42.219 --> 01:52:44.400
mixed with the natural properties of the swamp.

01:52:44.400 --> 01:52:48.369
There was no way of knowing what it would
do, whether it would still mutate a person,

01:52:48.369 --> 01:52:52.080
or whether the disease’s transformative
effect had been altered.

01:52:52.080 --> 01:52:55.380
But he had to know, and he was living on borrowed
time.

01:52:55.380 --> 01:52:59.869
Desperate, he raced to the nearest town, a
tiny community near the swamp.

01:52:59.869 --> 01:53:05.400
The first person, Alec told himself, the first
person he encountered would be his test subject.

01:53:05.400 --> 01:53:09.679
He had to see what effect this altered version
of the virus would do to a human being.

01:53:09.679 --> 01:53:14.590
It just so happened that the first person
he encountered was a little girl named Aé.

01:53:14.590 --> 01:53:19.540
And he wouldn’t live long enough to see
the full extent of what he was about to turn

01:53:19.540 --> 01:53:30.900
her into…

